{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git log --oneline -3; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD; nl -ba public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog'","aggregated_output":"cc5bf3ed docs(blog): UCF Part Two -- recast the resumption race as a gluing problem, not holonomy\n4bb2e195 docs(orchestrator): log UCF blog Part Three landing and codex architect review\n78ae1e69 docs(blog): UCF Part Three -- separate dependency closure from pending waits in the inherited-contract list (architect r2 nit)\ndiff --git a/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog b/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog\nindex 6291955f..f4a106d8 100644\n--- a/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog\n+++ b/public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog\n@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@\n         [:em \"code\"]\n         \" across lexical boundaries without accidental capture. Differential geometry taught physics how to move a \"\n         [:em \"vector\"]\n-        \" across a curved manifold without distortion. UCF is both at once: it is hygiene for suspended computation, and it is the connection that parallel-transports a CESK configuration across the distributed spacetime of nodes, runtimes, and checkpoints. The lease is where the curvature shows. A third part extends the transport past the boundary it was designed for: from one yin.vm to a different virtual machine altogether. The extension turned out to be narrow, and the path to seeing that it is narrow is recorded here, wrong turns included.\"],\n+        \" across a curved manifold without distortion. UCF is both at once: it is hygiene for suspended computation, and it is the connection that parallel-transports a CESK configuration across the distributed spacetime of nodes, runtimes, and checkpoints. Transport alone is not enough: a carrier that never consumes what it delivers hands the same checkpoint to every reader, and the lease is the gluing condition that makes one of those readings the computation. A third part extends the transport past the boundary it was designed for: from one yin.vm to a different virtual machine altogether. The extension turned out to be narrow, and the path to seeing that it is narrow is recorded here, wrong turns included.\"],\n        :content\n        [:section.blog-article\n         [:div.section-inner\n@@ -151,7 +151,9 @@\n            [:strong \"holonomy\"]\n            \", and its infinitesimal measure is \"\n            [:strong \"curvature\"]\n-           \". Curvature is the obstruction to a globally consistent notion of \\\"the same state everywhere.\\\"\"]\n+           \". Curvature is the obstruction to a globally consistent notion of \\\"the same state everywhere.\\\" Hold on to the word \"\n+           [:em \"section\"]\n+           \", because it is the one that will matter. Curvature is what goes wrong when the connection itself is path-dependent; there is a different and simpler way for \\\"the same state everywhere\\\" to fail, when a flat connection hands several readers one state each and nothing says which of the resulting sections is the real one. UCF's problem turns out to be the second kind.\"]\n           [:h3 \"The Bundle of Execution\"]\n           [:p \"Every term of that picture has a referent in datom.world, and the correspondence is exact enough to compute with.\"]\n           [:ul.bulleted\n@@ -179,17 +181,19 @@\n            \"A connection has one defining property beyond preserving content: it is defined pointwise along the path and depends on nothing global. UCF has the same property, and it is a stated invariant rather than an accident. The reference engine is \"\n            [:code \"yin.vm.semantic\"]\n            \", and a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from its canonical configuration, never by extending it. There is no global coordinate system. There is only the local lift at the emitter, the local lower at the resumer, and the connection that relates them.\"]\n-          [:h3 \"Where the Curvature Is\"]\n+          [:h3 \"Where Transport Stops Being Enough\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"If the manifold were flat, transport would be the end of the story. Lift, carry, lower, done. The reason UCF has a seventh section, longer than all the others, is that the manifold of distributed execution is curved, and the curvature has a name: \"\n+           \"If every checkpoint were read exactly once, transport would be the end of the story. Lift, carry, lower, done. The reason UCF has a seventh section, longer than all the others, is that nothing in the carrier guarantees a single reading, and the failure has a name: \"\n            [:strong \"the resumption race\"]\n            \".\"]\n           [:p\n            \"The emitter parks, lifts, appends the value to a medium, and keeps its local parked record. Three readers see the value on the stream. The emitter's own wait set wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines believe they may continue one computation. Nothing in dao.stream can stop them: a stream has no privileged reader and no destructive read.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"This is holonomy. Carry the state out along one path and back along another, and you do not get one state, you get several, each claiming to be the continuation of the same computation. Two resumers running the same checkpoint are two parallel-transported copies of one vector that have been brought back to the same point and found to disagree. The geometric content of \\\"split-brain\\\" is exactly that: transport around a loop is not the identity.\"]\n+           \"It is tempting to call this curvature, and the first draft of this essay did: carry the state around a loop and it comes back different. But that is not what happens. The connection is flat. Lift and lower are deterministic and do not depend on the path, and every one of the four machines holds a faithful copy of the same value. Nothing was distorted in transit. What went wrong is that the value was \"\n+           [:em \"copied\"]\n+           \", because a stream read does not consume, and now there are four candidate futures over one point in the computation's history where there must be one. In the language of sheaves this is a gluing problem, not a transport problem. Each reader holds a local section, a perfectly good continuation over its own neighbourhood. The question is which local sections may be glued into the one global section that is the computation's world line, and a connection has no opinion on that. Something else has to supply the compatibility condition.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"UCF does not pretend the manifold is flat. It measures the curvature and fences it:\"]\n+           \"UCF does not pretend the readers will sort it out among themselves. It supplies the gluing condition, and makes it the only one:\"]\n           [:ul.bulleted\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Content is not occurrence.\"]\n@@ -197,7 +201,7 @@\n             [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n             \" is content identity, for deduplication and materialization. \"\n             [:code \":yin.k/occurrence\"]\n-            \" is the thing custody names, minted exactly once when a park is first exported, carried unchanged by every snapshot variant and every retry. In geometric terms: the vector has many coordinate expressions, but one identity in the bundle.\"]\n+            \" is the thing custody names, minted exactly once when a park is first exported, carried unchanged by every snapshot variant and every retry. In geometric terms: the vector has many coordinate expressions, but one identity in the bundle, and the occurrence is the point over which the candidate sections must be glued.\"]\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Resumption is a take, a take is a write, exclusion is a lease.\"]\n             \" Custody is arbitrated above the stream on an arbitration medium, a dao.space whose facts are ordinary datoms, using the dao.lease vocabulary unchanged. The leased subject is the occurrence. Only grantor-authored facts establish terms. The grantor is the transactor of the occurrence's execution ledger, the one boundary that actually possesses the right to admit effects as this occurrence.\"]\n@@ -210,14 +214,14 @@\n             [:strong \"The absence of a lapse is never evidence of tenure.\"]\n             \" The first draft tried to fence by having consumers look for \"\n             [:code \":lapsed\"]\n-            \" records and drop effects whose incarnation had one. That is correlation, not fencing: a partitioned consumer cannot see the lapse, and accepts stale effects. The epoch check at commitment replaces it. In the geometric reading, you cannot determine that two transported vectors agree by checking that nobody has yet reported them disagreeing. You need a gauge, and the epoch is the gauge.\"]\n+            \" records and drop effects whose incarnation had one. That is correlation, not fencing: a partitioned consumer cannot see the lapse, and accepts stale effects. The epoch check at commitment replaces it. In the geometric reading, two local sections do not glue because nobody has yet reported that they conflict; they glue because a compatibility condition says so, and the condition must be checkable by the one who commits. The epoch is that condition, issued by the authority and carried on every effect: not a measurement of anything, but the term under which a candidate is admitted as the continuation.\"]\n            [:li\n             [:strong \"Causal linearity is a chain of edges.\"]\n             \" Completion of a checkpoint is not the holder's report; it is the grantor's ledger transition that atomically records the lapse, the occurrence-completed fact, and exactly one successor or terminal edge. A successor published without an accepted report is an orphan, queryable as history but never a grant. The world line of a computation is thereby a single path through occurrences, and the ledger is what makes it single.\"]]\n           [:p\n            \"Notice what UCF does \"\n            [:em \"not\"]\n-           \" do. It does not make the manifold flat by fiat. It does not promise exactly-once effects through a bare stream; a composition that needs them must wire a fenced writer and an enrolled consumer, and one that cannot reach the authority must suspend protected commitment or declare at-least-once. A composition that cannot wire a dao.space transactor as grantor cannot offer exclusive custody at all; it composes fork semantics only. The specification calls this an honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over. Geometry agrees: you cannot remove curvature by choosing better coordinates. You can only measure it and account for it.\"]\n+           \" do. It does not make the carrier consume what it delivers, and it does not pretend that reading is taking. It does not promise exactly-once effects through a bare stream; a composition that needs them must wire a fenced writer and an enrolled consumer, and one that cannot reach the authority must suspend protected commitment or declare at-least-once. A composition that cannot wire a dao.space transactor as grantor cannot offer exclusive custody at all; it composes fork semantics only. The specification calls this an honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over. Geometry agrees: local sections do not glue themselves. Without a gluing condition you have a family of candidates, not a global section, and no choice of coordinates will turn one into the other.\"]\n           [:h2 \"Why the Two Parallels Are One\"]\n           [:p\n            \"Hygiene and parallel transport look like different ideas from different fields. They are the same idea seen at two scales.\"]\n@@ -230,11 +234,11 @@\n           [:p\n            \"Parallel transport is the \"\n            [:em \"global\"]\n-           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined connection does not give a globally consistent identification of states when the base is curved, and the failure is measured by holonomy around loops. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They make the path \"\n+           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined, perfectly flat connection gives you one faithful copy per reader, and when the carrier does not consume, that is several candidate sections over one point with nothing to choose between them. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They are the gluing condition that makes the world line \"\n            [:strong \"single\"]\n-           \": they ensure that of all the loops the value might be carried around, exactly one closes as the continuation of the computation, and every other closure is distinguishable as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n+           \": of all the candidate continuations over an occurrence, exactly one is admitted as the computation, and every other is distinguishable afterwards as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree with no loops. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and the manifold they live on has nontrivial topology: partitions, retries, crash restarts, redelivery. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n+           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree: one expansion per site, nothing to glue. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and partitions, retries, crash restarts, and redelivery multiply the readers. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n           [:h2 \"Part Three: Transport to a Different Machine\"]\n           [:p\n            \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways. One qualifier governs everything below: these are design decisions, not landed code. What ships today is the nested value grammar of Part One; the tuple form described here is the next specification change.\"]\n@@ -331,7 +335,7 @@\n            [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n            \" is that lesson, with the one necessary refinement that content identity and occurrence identity must be kept apart. Lisp taught, through forty years of macro pain, that a name is not a meaning until it carries its binding context. UCF's primitive markers, contract stamps, and content hashes are that lesson applied to a runtime instead of a reader. Just-in-time compilers taught that compiled code can be abandoned mid-flight and the interpreter's frame rebuilt from metadata kept at safepoints, and that the reverse, on-stack replacement, enters compiled code at those same points. Part Three's lift is deoptimization across a machine boundary, and its lower is analogous to on-stack replacement, though it may simply start a fresh native task from the canonical frame rather than enter a running activation; the tuple set is the interpreter frame they meet at.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"And differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with, and that on a curved space the best connection in the world still leaves holonomy around loops which you must measure rather than wish away. UCF's seventh section is that lesson, written in datoms.\"]\n+           \"And geometry taught two things. Differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with. Sheaf theory taught that local pieces, each correct on its own patch, do not add up to a global object unless a gluing condition says how they agree on the overlaps, and that the condition is extra structure which nothing in the pieces supplies. UCF's seventh section is the second lesson, written in datoms: the connection is Parts One and Three, and the ledger is the gluing.\"]\n           [:p\n            \"A continuation is a value, not a session. That is the stance at the head of the UCF specification, and everything else follows from taking it seriously. A value can be content-addressed, so it can be deduplicated and verified. A value can be carried by anything, so no carrier is privileged. A value can be read by anyone, so custody must be arbitrated above the carrier by a boundary that actually possesses something. Hygiene makes the value mean the same thing everywhere. The lease makes sure it runs in only one place at a time. Between them, a suspended computation becomes a vector in a bundle over the distributed world, and UCF is the connection that lets it travel.\"]\n           [:p [:strong \"Learn more:\"]]\n     1\t#:blog{:title\n     2\t       \"Hygienic Execution State: The Universal Continuation Format as Parallel Transport\",\n     3\t       :date #inst \"2026-10-09T00:00:00.000-00:00\",\n     4\t       :abstract\n     5\t       [:p\n     6\t        \"What is the Universal Continuation Format really doing? Two older ideas answer the question from opposite directions. Hygienic macro systems taught Lisp how to move \"\n     7\t        [:em \"code\"]\n     8\t        \" across lexical boundaries without accidental capture. Differential geometry taught physics how to move a \"\n     9\t        [:em \"vector\"]\n    10\t        \" across a curved manifold without distortion. UCF is both at once: it is hygiene for suspended computation, and it is the connection that parallel-transports a CESK configuration across the distributed spacetime of nodes, runtimes, and checkpoints. Transport alone is not enough: a carrier that never consumes what it delivers hands the same checkpoint to every reader, and the lease is the gluing condition that makes one of those readings the computation. A third part extends the transport past the boundary it was designed for: from one yin.vm to a different virtual machine altogether. The extension turned out to be narrow, and the path to seeing that it is narrow is recorded here, wrong turns included.\"],\n    11\t       :content\n    12\t       [:section.blog-article\n    13\t        [:div.section-inner\n    14\t         [:article\n    15\t          :$blog-title\n    16\t          :$blog-date\n    17\t          [:h2 \"The Question\"]\n    18\t          [:p\n    19\t           \"A continuation in yin.vm is a value. A parked configuration is a record \"\n    20\t           [:code \"{segment pc env stack k}\"]\n    21\t           \" plus a reason and some resource ids. There is no handle in it, no closure object, no thread. On its own machine it is complete: the VM can pick it up and keep going.\"]\n    22\t          [:p\n    23\t           \"On any other machine it means nothing. The segment is a tempid minted by one transactor. The stream ids are keys into one VM's store. The environment may hold host functions. Nothing in the record says which primitives it assumed, which opcode table it was compiled against, or which version of \"\n    24\t           [:code \"resolve-var\"]\n    25\t           \" gave its symbols their meaning. The specification puts it bluntly: the record is \"\n    26\t           [:em \"locally complete and globally meaningless\"]\n    27\t           \".\"]\n    28\t          [:p\n    29\t           \"The Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the lift of that local record into a self-describing value that any conforming engine can lower back into its own registers, together with the discipline that governs who may lower it. That is the mechanical description. This essay is about what the mechanism \"\n    30\t           [:em \"is\"]\n    31\t           \", seen from two older disciplines that solved the same problem for different objects.\"]\n    32\t          [:h2 \"Part One: The Lexical Parallel\"]\n    33\t          [:h3 \"The Capture Problem\"]\n    34\t          [:p\n    35\t           \"Early Lisp macros were textual rewriters with a dangerous freedom. A macro expanded into code, and that code landed inside whatever lexical scope the call site happened to have. If the macro introduced a temporary named \"\n    36\t           [:code \"tmp\"]\n    37\t           \" and the caller already had a \"\n    38\t           [:code \"tmp\"]\n    39\t           \", the two collided. If the macro referred to \"\n    40\t           [:code \"list\"]\n    41\t           \" and the caller had shadowed \"\n    42\t           [:code \"list\"]\n    43\t           \" with a local, the macro silently called the wrong thing. The code fragment carried its text but not its meaning, and meaning was assigned by the host scope it fell into.\"]\n    44\t          [:p\n    45\t           \"This is \"\n    46\t           [:strong \"unhygienic expansion\"]\n    47\t           \": identifiers are bare names, and bare names resolve wherever they land.\"]\n    48\t          [:h3 \"What Hygiene Does\"]\n    49\t          [:p\n    50\t           \"Kohlbecker's algorithm, the Clinger and Rees explicit-renaming systems, and Dybvig's \"\n    51\t           [:code \"syntax-case\"]\n    52\t           \" all converge on one move. A syntax object is not a bare symbol. It is a symbol \"\n    53\t           [:em \"plus its lexical context\"]\n    54\t           \": a set of marks, scopes, or renaming environments that record where the identifier was born. When the fragment is transplanted into a new scope, the resolver consults the carried context, not the ambient one. An identifier introduced by the macro resolves in the macro's definition environment. An identifier supplied by the caller resolves in the caller's. The two cannot be confused because each carries its own origin.\"]\n    55\t          [:p\n    56\t           \"Hygiene is therefore not a restriction on what macros may do. It is a change in what an identifier \"\n    57\t           [:em \"is\"]\n    58\t           \". A name becomes a binding with provenance, and provenance travels with the fragment.\"]\n    59\t          [:h3 \"Unhygienic Continuation Transfer\"]\n    60\t          [:p\n    61\t           \"Now replace the code fragment with a suspended computation, and the lexical boundary with a machine boundary. Every capture failure of the macro world has an exact runtime twin:\"]\n    62\t          [:ul.bulleted\n    63\t           [:li\n    64\t            [:strong \"Identifier capture becomes pointer capture.\"]\n    65\t            \" The record names \"\n    66\t            [:code \":segment 123\"]\n    67\t            \". On the receiving machine, \"\n    68\t            [:code \"123\"]\n    69\t            \" is different code or no code. A continuation that names code by tempid is not a value; it is a pointer into a heap that did not travel.\"]\n    70\t           [:li\n    71\t            [:strong \"Shadowing becomes store pollution.\"]\n    72\t            \" Resolution in yin.vm runs environment, then store, then primitives. If the migrated slice were merged into the receiver's store, a receiver that happened to bind \"\n    73\t            [:code \"+\"]\n    74\t            \" would change what the migrated program computes, with no collision at all. The first UCF draft said in two places that the slice both wins on collision and merges under the local store. That contradiction was the shadowing bug, rediscovered.\"]\n    75\t           [:li\n    76\t            [:strong \"Free variables become unportable handles.\"]\n    77\t            \" A host function in the environment, a live stream handle, the FFI pair: these are references into one process's address space. Carried naively, they are dangling pointers dressed as values.\"]\n    78\t           [:li\n    79\t            [:strong \"Marker forgery becomes data ambiguity.\"]\n    80\t            \" The first draft encoded closures as ordinary maps like \"\n    81\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/primitive '+}\"]\n    82\t            \". But a program may legitimately contain that very map as data. A decoder could not tell the encoder's marker from the program's literal, exactly as an unhygienic expander cannot tell the macro's \"\n    83\t            [:code \"tmp\"]\n    84\t            \" from the caller's.\"]]\n    85\t          [:h3 \"Hygienic Execution State\"]\n    86\t          [:p\n    87\t           \"UCF's answer is the hygiene move, applied to every component of the configuration. Each answer attaches context so that the value resolves against its origin rather than against wherever it lands.\"]\n    88\t          [:ul.bulleted\n    89\t           [:li\n    90\t            [:strong \"Code carries its identity, not its address.\"]\n    91\t            \" Every segment a continuation may reference bears \"\n    92\t            [:code \":yin.code/hash\"]\n    93\t            \", the content address of its canonical instruction vector: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, operands saturated with the loader's defaults, provenance and entity ids excluded. Two segments address equally if and only if their loader-resolved interpretations are equal. The name says \"\n    94\t            [:em \"what the code is\"]\n    95\t            \", so it means the same thing on every machine that can hash.\"]\n    96\t           [:li\n    97\t            [:strong \"Semantics carries a stamp.\"]\n    98\t            \" An address fixes which instructions; it does not fix what \"\n    99\t            [:code \":call\"]\n   100\t            \" means. So the value carries \"\n   101\t            [:code \":yin.k/contract\"]\n   102\t            \", naming one published revision of the complete execution contract: opcode table, transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, scheduler semantics. A resumer on a different revision returns \"\n   103\t            [:code \":yin.k/profile-mismatch\"]\n   104\t            \" before lowering. This is the definition-site environment of the macro, made explicit as a version.\"]\n   105\t           [:li\n   106\t            [:strong \"The grammar is disjoint by construction.\"]\n   107\t            \" Every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker. A program's own maps are always wrapped as \"\n   108\t            [:code \":yin.k/literal\"]\n   109\t            \" with encoded entries. No decoder decision depends on program content, exactly as a hygienic resolver never asks whether a symbol \\\"looks like\\\" a macro temporary. The marks are structural, not heuristic.\"]\n   110\t           [:li\n   111\t            [:strong \"The store slice is isolated, never merged.\"]\n   112\t            \" The resumed task runs in a fresh execution store whose contents are exactly the carried reachable slice, with the remapped resource cells and the resumer's own FFI pair held in a private resource table beside it rather than inside it. Nothing of the receiver's participates. A name absent from the slice resolves through primitives and modules exactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to intervene. This is the renaming environment: the transplanted computation sees its own scope and only its own scope.\"]\n   113\t           [:li\n   114\t            [:strong \"Primitives are bindings, not meanings.\"]\n   115\t            \" A primitive travels as \"\n   116\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/primitive :yin.k/name sym}\"]\n   117\t            \", and the name is resolved at the resumer under a profile check. A symbol in the environment that referred to a host function is recorded by reverse lookup against the emitter's primitive table, and refused if no portable binding exists. The hygienic system's rule that an identifier is a name plus a binding context is here verbatim.\"]\n   118\t           [:li\n   119\t            [:strong \"Anything else is refused, totally and by path.\"]\n   120\t            \" Encoding is one total function from value and path to outcome. A live handle, a host object, a closure over a non-portable value: each is \"\n   121\t            [:code \":yin.k/non-portable\"]\n   122\t            \" naming the path where it was found. The lift never produces a half-hygienic value.\"]]\n   123\t          [:p\n   124\t           \"The structural resemblance is not a metaphor. A hygienic macro system and UCF are solving the same problem on the same abstract object: a fragment that must mean the same thing after transplantation as it did before. The macro system does it for a fragment of syntax entering a new lexical scope. UCF does it for a fragment of execution entering a new machine. Both work by refusing bare names and insisting that every reference carry the context that gives it meaning.\"]\n   125\t          [:h2 \"Part Two: The Geometric Parallel\"]\n   126\t          [:h3 \"Moving a Vector on a Curved Surface\"]\n   127\t          [:p\n   128\t           \"In differential geometry, a vector lives in the tangent space at a point. It does not exist \\\"everywhere\\\"; it exists \"\n   129\t           [:em \"at\"]\n   130\t           \" a point. To compare a vector at one point with a vector at another, you must transport one of them along a path. On a flat surface this is trivial: slide it and keep it parallel to itself. On a curved surface there is no canonical way to keep a vector \\\"the same\\\" as it moves, because the tangent spaces at different points are different vector spaces with no built-in identification.\"]\n   131\t          [:p\n   132\t           \"The structure that supplies the identification is a \"\n   133\t           [:strong \"connection\"]\n   134\t           \", written \"\n   135\t           [:code \"∇\"]\n   136\t           \". A connection says, for each infinitesimal step along a path, how the tangent space at the new point is identified with the one just left. Parallel transport along a curve \"\n   137\t           [:code \"γ\"]\n   138\t           \" is the rule \"\n   139\t           [:code \"∇_γ̇ v = 0\"]\n   140\t           \": the covariant derivative of the vector along the curve vanishes. The vector changes coordinates as it moves, because the coordinates at each point differ, but its intrinsic content is preserved by the connection.\"]\n   141\t          [:p\n   142\t           \"The general picture is a \"\n   143\t           [:strong \"fiber bundle\"]\n   144\t           \". There is a base manifold \"\n   145\t           [:code \"M\"]\n   146\t           \" of points. Over each point \"\n   147\t           [:code \"x\"]\n   148\t           \" sits a fiber \"\n   149\t           [:code \"F_x\"]\n   150\t           \", a copy of some space of states. The total space is all fibers glued together. A section picks one state per point. A connection says how to carry a state from one fiber to a neighboring fiber. And the deep fact is this: carrying a state around a closed loop and returning to the start does not in general give back the same state. The failure to return is \"\n   151\t           [:strong \"holonomy\"]\n   152\t           \", and its infinitesimal measure is \"\n   153\t           [:strong \"curvature\"]\n   154\t           \". Curvature is the obstruction to a globally consistent notion of \\\"the same state everywhere.\\\" Hold on to the word \"\n   155\t           [:em \"section\"]\n   156\t           \", because it is the one that will matter. Curvature is what goes wrong when the connection itself is path-dependent; there is a different and simpler way for \\\"the same state everywhere\\\" to fail, when a flat connection hands several readers one state each and nothing says which of the resulting sections is the real one. UCF's problem turns out to be the second kind.\"]\n   157\t          [:h3 \"The Bundle of Execution\"]\n   158\t          [:p \"Every term of that picture has a referent in datom.world, and the correspondence is exact enough to compute with.\"]\n   159\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   160\t           [:li\n   161\t            [:strong \"Base manifold M: distributed spacetime.\"]\n   162\t            \" The points are places a computation can be: this JVM, that Node.js process, a Dart isolate on a phone, a checkpoint persisted at a particular transaction time. Points are separated both in space (different hosts) and in time (a park at t₁, a resume at t₂). The manifold is not flat: hosts differ in codec, in number representation, in which primitives exist, in which code is already loaded.\"]\n   163\t           [:li\n   164\t            [:strong \"Fiber F_x: the local CESK state space.\"]\n   165\t            \" Over each point sits the space of configurations \"\n   166\t            [:code \"⟨C, E, S, K⟩\"]\n   167\t            \" that machine can hold. Control, environment, store, continuation. These fibers are isomorphic in the abstract (every conforming engine lowers into the canonical CESK), but they are not \"\n   168\t            [:em \"identical\"]\n   169\t            \": each has its own local ids, its own store keys, its own resource table, its own host representations. Exactly as tangent spaces at different points are each ℝⁿ yet have no canonical identification, two VMs each hold CESK configurations yet have no canonical map between their segment ids.\"]\n   170\t           [:li\n   171\t            [:strong \"Path γ: the dao.stream.\"]\n   172\t            \" The curve along which a suspended computation moves is a stream. It may be a file, a socket, a DHT entry, an append log. The specification calls this role the \"\n   173\t            [:em \"carrier\"]\n   174\t            \" and says of it: sees opaque data, holds nothing. That is exactly what a path in the base is. A path does not carry meaning. It carries position.\"]\n   175\t           [:li\n   176\t            [:strong \"Connection ∇: the Universal Continuation Format.\"]\n   177\t            \" UCF is the rule that identifies a configuration in the fiber over the emitter with a configuration in the fiber over the resumer. The lift takes a local configuration to a point-independent representative: code by content hash, primitives by name plus profile, store as an isolated slice, markers in a disjoint grammar. The lower takes that representative back into the resumer's local coordinates: fresh local ids minted, resources remapped into a private table, references re-sealed under the receiver's own capability secret. Content is invariant; coordinates change. That is \"\n   178\t            [:code \"∇_γ̇ v = 0\"]\n   179\t            \" for execution state.\"]]\n   180\t          [:p\n   181\t           \"A connection has one defining property beyond preserving content: it is defined pointwise along the path and depends on nothing global. UCF has the same property, and it is a stated invariant rather than an accident. The reference engine is \"\n   182\t           [:code \"yin.vm.semantic\"]\n   183\t           \", and a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from its canonical configuration, never by extending it. There is no global coordinate system. There is only the local lift at the emitter, the local lower at the resumer, and the connection that relates them.\"]\n   184\t          [:h3 \"Where Transport Stops Being Enough\"]\n   185\t          [:p\n   186\t           \"If every checkpoint were read exactly once, transport would be the end of the story. Lift, carry, lower, done. The reason UCF has a seventh section, longer than all the others, is that nothing in the carrier guarantees a single reading, and the failure has a name: \"\n   187\t           [:strong \"the resumption race\"]\n   188\t           \".\"]\n   189\t          [:p\n   190\t           \"The emitter parks, lifts, appends the value to a medium, and keeps its local parked record. Three readers see the value on the stream. The emitter's own wait set wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines believe they may continue one computation. Nothing in dao.stream can stop them: a stream has no privileged reader and no destructive read.\"]\n   191\t          [:p\n   192\t           \"It is tempting to call this curvature, and the first draft of this essay did: carry the state around a loop and it comes back different. But that is not what happens. The connection is flat. Lift and lower are deterministic and do not depend on the path, and every one of the four machines holds a faithful copy of the same value. Nothing was distorted in transit. What went wrong is that the value was \"\n   193\t           [:em \"copied\"]\n   194\t           \", because a stream read does not consume, and now there are four candidate futures over one point in the computation's history where there must be one. In the language of sheaves this is a gluing problem, not a transport problem. Each reader holds a local section, a perfectly good continuation over its own neighbourhood. The question is which local sections may be glued into the one global section that is the computation's world line, and a connection has no opinion on that. Something else has to supply the compatibility condition.\"]\n   195\t          [:p\n   196\t           \"UCF does not pretend the readers will sort it out among themselves. It supplies the gluing condition, and makes it the only one:\"]\n   197\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   198\t           [:li\n   199\t            [:strong \"Content is not occurrence.\"]\n   200\t            \" The same parked computation may be encoded more than once with different sharing thresholds, so the encodings have different content addresses while remaining one checkpoint occurrence. \"\n   201\t            [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n   202\t            \" is content identity, for deduplication and materialization. \"\n   203\t            [:code \":yin.k/occurrence\"]\n   204\t            \" is the thing custody names, minted exactly once when a park is first exported, carried unchanged by every snapshot variant and every retry. In geometric terms: the vector has many coordinate expressions, but one identity in the bundle, and the occurrence is the point over which the candidate sections must be glued.\"]\n   205\t           [:li\n   206\t            [:strong \"Resumption is a take, a take is a write, exclusion is a lease.\"]\n   207\t            \" Custody is arbitrated above the stream on an arbitration medium, a dao.space whose facts are ordinary datoms, using the dao.lease vocabulary unchanged. The leased subject is the occurrence. Only grantor-authored facts establish terms. The grantor is the transactor of the occurrence's execution ledger, the one boundary that actually possesses the right to admit effects as this occurrence.\"]\n   208\t           [:li\n   209\t            [:strong \"Epochs are the fence.\"]\n   210\t            \" The ledger carries an epoch datom for each occurrence, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim. A resumed incarnation emits through a fenced writer that stamps each effect with its lease, its epoch, and a stable operation id \"\n   211\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}\"]\n   212\t            \". An enrolled consumer checks the epoch, compares the operation's canonical intent against its durable dedup record, and commits the side effect, all in one atomic boundary. A stale epoch is refused at commitment. A lapsed holder is never told to stop; it stops at its own lease bound, and what it did past the bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\"]\n   213\t           [:li\n   214\t            [:strong \"The absence of a lapse is never evidence of tenure.\"]\n   215\t            \" The first draft tried to fence by having consumers look for \"\n   216\t            [:code \":lapsed\"]\n   217\t            \" records and drop effects whose incarnation had one. That is correlation, not fencing: a partitioned consumer cannot see the lapse, and accepts stale effects. The epoch check at commitment replaces it. In the geometric reading, two local sections do not glue because nobody has yet reported that they conflict; they glue because a compatibility condition says so, and the condition must be checkable by the one who commits. The epoch is that condition, issued by the authority and carried on every effect: not a measurement of anything, but the term under which a candidate is admitted as the continuation.\"]\n   218\t           [:li\n   219\t            [:strong \"Causal linearity is a chain of edges.\"]\n   220\t            \" Completion of a checkpoint is not the holder's report; it is the grantor's ledger transition that atomically records the lapse, the occurrence-completed fact, and exactly one successor or terminal edge. A successor published without an accepted report is an orphan, queryable as history but never a grant. The world line of a computation is thereby a single path through occurrences, and the ledger is what makes it single.\"]]\n   221\t          [:p\n   222\t           \"Notice what UCF does \"\n   223\t           [:em \"not\"]\n   224\t           \" do. It does not make the carrier consume what it delivers, and it does not pretend that reading is taking. It does not promise exactly-once effects through a bare stream; a composition that needs them must wire a fenced writer and an enrolled consumer, and one that cannot reach the authority must suspend protected commitment or declare at-least-once. A composition that cannot wire a dao.space transactor as grantor cannot offer exclusive custody at all; it composes fork semantics only. The specification calls this an honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over. Geometry agrees: local sections do not glue themselves. Without a gluing condition you have a family of candidates, not a global section, and no choice of coordinates will turn one into the other.\"]\n   225\t          [:h2 \"Why the Two Parallels Are One\"]\n   226\t          [:p\n   227\t           \"Hygiene and parallel transport look like different ideas from different fields. They are the same idea seen at two scales.\"]\n   228\t          [:p\n   229\t           \"Hygiene is the \"\n   230\t           [:em \"fiberwise\"]\n   231\t           \" story. It says: a value in a fiber must carry enough context that the lower, in a different fiber, resolves every reference against the origin rather than the destination. Content-addressed code, stamped contracts, isolated stores, disjoint tagged markers, named primitives. These are the marks and scopes of the syntax object, placed on execution state. They make the connection \"\n   232\t           [:strong \"well defined\"]\n   233\t           \": given a lifted value, there is exactly one configuration it lowers to on any conforming engine.\"]\n   234\t          [:p\n   235\t           \"Parallel transport is the \"\n   236\t           [:em \"global\"]\n   237\t           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined, perfectly flat connection gives you one faithful copy per reader, and when the carrier does not consume, that is several candidate sections over one point with nothing to choose between them. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They are the gluing condition that makes the world line \"\n   238\t           [:strong \"single\"]\n   239\t           \": of all the candidate continuations over an occurrence, exactly one is admitted as the computation, and every other is distinguishable afterwards as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n   240\t          [:p\n   241\t           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree: one expansion per site, nothing to glue. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and partitions, retries, crash restarts, and redelivery multiply the readers. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n   242\t          [:h2 \"Part Three: Transport to a Different Machine\"]\n   243\t          [:p\n   244\t           \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways. One qualifier governs everything below: these are design decisions, not landed code. What ships today is the nested value grammar of Part One; the tuple form described here is the next specification change.\"]\n   245\t          [:h3 \"What the Specification Already Says\"]\n   246\t          [:p\n   247\t           \"UCF has one sentence on the subject, and it turns out to be the whole rule: \"\n   248\t           [:em \"a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from the canonical configuration, never by extending it.\"]\n   249\t           \" The specification's foreign engine is a WebAssembly lowering, a JIT, a register allocator: an engine that runs yin's own instruction vector in a physical layout of its choosing, and publishes a static safepoint map from its slots back to canonical ones. A genuinely different VM adds one thing to that: a runtime and value domain of its own. It may well run compiled code and keep intermediates in registers, as a JIT already does; what is new is that it represents a yin integer, string, or map in whatever way its own runtime prefers, while still owing the canonical instruction semantics. The sentence still holds. The work is in saying what it costs.\"]\n   250\t          [:h3 \"The First Wrong Turn: Lift to the AST\"]\n   251\t          [:p\n   252\t           \"The first proposal was to raise the safepoint one level. A safepoint today is a pair \"\n   253\t           [:code \"(segment-hash, pc)\"]\n   254\t           \": a content-addressed instruction vector and an index into it. That looked yin-specific, so the proposal was to identify safepoints by Universal AST node instead, treat the AST as the shared structure every VM compiles from, and describe each VM's state space as derived from it through its own compiler. In the geometric language this was a principal bundle whose fiber is the AST, with each VM's state space an associated bundle, and transport induced from the AST level down.\"]\n   255\t          [:p\n   256\t           \"It was rejected for a reason that was already in the specification. The Universal AST is the canonical source code, and the semantic VM's instruction vector is its \"\n   257\t           [:em \"deterministic lowering\"]\n   258\t           \" under a published lowering profile: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, operands saturated with the loader's defaults. It is not a pointwise bijection with AST nodes; an application or a conditional lowers to several instructions, lambda bodies are placed out of line, and provenance back to the tree is a separate side table. That is fine, because the safepoint does not need the bijection. The parking transitions are transitions of the \"\n   259\t           [:em \"resolved instruction stream\"]\n   260\t           \" under the execution contract, and a pair \"\n   261\t           [:code \"(segment-hash, pc)\"]\n   262\t           \" names one of them exactly. Naming the AST node instead would mean naming a point that may correspond to several instructions, only some of which park. The owner's ruling that the canonical code form is the positional vector, not entity-attribute-value, was made for code identity and execution order; the safepoints into that code inherit it. Cross-VM safepoints stay at the semantic VM level. There is one fiber, the canonical CESK, and a VM is a coordinate chart on it, not a different bundle.\"]\n   263\t          [:h3 \"Narrowing: the Problem Is the Values\"]\n   264\t          [:p\n   265\t           \"With code identity settled, what is actually not canonical across machines? Control is solved: the segment hash plus the contract stamp fix both which instructions and what they mean. The \"\n   266\t           [:em \"structure\"]\n   267\t           \" of the continuation is solved: a frame is a canonical shape, a return pc with a stack base and a captured environment, and every conforming engine must produce that shape. What a native VM represents in its own domain is every component that holds a \"\n   268\t           [:em \"value\"]\n   269\t           \": the environment, the store, the operand stack, and transitively the environments captured inside frames.\"]\n   270\t          [:p\n   271\t           \"A note on the operand stack, since the classic CESK machine has no such thing. In Felleisen's formulation the continuation \"\n   272\t           [:em \"is\"]\n   273\t           \" the stack: a list of frames, each remembering what to do with the next value, with already-evaluated arguments held inside the frames. Linearization flattens the AST into push and call instructions, and the values that the AST-walking machine kept inside frames move onto a flat vector the semantic VM calls \"\n   274\t           [:code \"St\"]\n   275\t           \". An ordinary return frame then keeps a stack base marking its slice of that shared vector rather than holding the values itself; an effect frame may additionally snapshot the stack it needs. Operationally, then, \"\n   276\t           [:code \"St\"]\n   277\t           \" is where linearization put the work an AST walker holds in its application continuations, not an addition to CESK; the semantic VM files it under control because it is part of what the machine is doing, not part of what it has bound. For transport it adds no new problem; it is simply the place a compiling VM is most likely to have put a value in a register.\"]\n   278\t          [:p\n   279\t           \"So the cross-VM problem is narrow: normalize the value-holding components, and separately make sure a compiled engine can produce them at a safepoint at all. The second is a deoptimization obligation in the JIT sense, and we return to it. The first is the question of a normal form.\"]\n   280\t          [:h3 \"Does CBOR Normalize Them?\"]\n   281\t          [:p\n   282\t           \"The tempting answer is that the wire codec already does. DaoJing stores canonical CBOR: every host produces the same bytes for the same integers of any width, the same float64, the same strings, keywords, vectors, sets, and maps. Three VMs already agree at this layer, since the JVM, Node, and Dart ports share one Jing. Is that the normalization?\"]\n   283\t          [:p\n   284\t           \"It is one of three layers, and the bottom one. CBOR's data model is trees of plain data. The environment, store, and operand stack are not trees of plain data; they are a graph of machine state with identity, with references into the machine, and with references out of it. Each thing in that gap is something no codec can express, and the specification's value grammar is the list of them: a closure points at code and at an environment, so it carries a content hash and an entry pc rather than a tempid; the configuration is a DAG, so sharing is made explicit in a value table rather than duplicated; two cursor references at one position may be independently advanceable, so identity is carried as an emitter-minted cell that is deliberately \"\n   285\t           [:em \"not\"]\n   286\t           \" content-addressed; a cycle is refused; a host function becomes a name resolved under a profile; a stream handle becomes a descriptor the exporter must be able to serve; anything else is refused by path. CBOR carries all of these as ordinary maps and knows nothing of what they mean, and its agreement across hosts holds for Jing's admitted portable domain, with host-capability refusals at its edge. Above both layers sits a third: whether the receiving VM \"\n   287\t           [:em \"computes\"]\n   288\t           \" on the decoded values the way yin does. A sixty-four bit integer that stays exact, a map key equality that agrees, a negated zero that keeps its sign. Bytes and reference shape do not establish that. The grammar's primitive rule reaches toward it, by checking semantic profiles so that only compatible bindings resolve at both ends; what proves it is behavioral parity, running the same programs to the same safepoints on both engines and comparing.\"]\n   289\t          [:h3 \"The Decision: the Transported State Is a Tuple Set\"]\n   290\t          [:p\n   291\t           \"The value grammar's seven rules were written for a nested wire form, markers inside markers. The observation that changed the design was that if the transported state is represented as \"\n   292\t           [:em \"tuples\"]\n   293\t           \", most of those rules stop being special cases. In a tuple store a value is an entity, and two tuples that reference the same entity \"\n   294\t           [:em \"are\"]\n   295\t           \" sharing; the value table disappears. If value positions hold only scalars and entity references, with a program's own map literal becoming an entity of its own, then there is no map at any value position and the marker-forgery problem disappears by construction, under an explicit schema that fixes entity kinds, valid attributes, and reference completeness. A closure is an entity with a segment hash, an entry pc, and a reference to its environment entity. Cycles are the one rule that does not move: tuples can express a reference back to an earlier entity, but the canonical value domain still excludes cycles, because the reference machine cannot produce one and because an immutable cycle has no well-defined per-entity content address. Cycle refusal stays; admitting cycles would be a separate ruling on semantics, addressing, and lowering, and it is deferred. And lowering becomes loading: the tuple set is read through a validating loader into an isolated task, with its program store, its private resource table, its module stores, and its control and scheduler state reconstructed as distinct components, in the same spirit as the code loader's projection path.\"]\n   296\t          [:p\n   297\t           \"What tuples do not dissolve is exactly the residue that was always the real content of the grammar. An entity id is a tempid, local to one machine, the same problem as \"\n   298\t           [:code \":segment 123\"]\n   299\t           \". For an immutable value the entity is content-addressed and deduplication is free. For a cursor cell it must not be, because two cells at one position are distinct, so the id travels as a tempid and is remapped on lowering. This is the specification's separation of content identity from occurrence identity, pushed down to every entity: \"\n   300\t           [:strong \"each entity is either content, hash-addressed and shareable, or identity, carried as a tempid and remapped, and the schema says which.\"]\n   301\t           \" That is the identity discipline, and it is necessary rather than complete. Aliasing survives only under an allocation rule: every source cell gets one transport identity, every reference to it reuses that identity, independent cells keep distinct identities, and lowering allocates exactly one private cell per identity. Content hashing must respect those identities, so two immutable values wrapping two independent cells at the same position never deduplicate into one. Around the discipline the rest of the grammar stays: a disjoint scalar-or-reference value grammar, schema validation of correctly hashed input, reachability checks, and the authentication of resource references before lift and their re-sealing on lower. Tuples remove marker ambiguity; they do not remove forged capabilities, and the two should not be confused. References out of the machine are unchanged: a primitive is still a name under a profile, a stream is still a descriptor, a host object is still refused. Totality is unchanged: the lift either produces the full tuple set or refuses naming the entity and attribute. And equality needs defining: Jing hashes the bytes of whatever value it is given and sorts map keys and set elements, but it preserves vector order and knows nothing of entity references or tempid naming, so the transport schema must fix a canonical representation of the tuple set and the identity renaming under which two sets count as equal. Jing then supplies deterministic bytes and a hash.\"]\n   302\t          [:p\n   303\t           \"This is consistent with the ruling against entity-attribute-value for code. Code was fixed as a positional vector because sequence is its content and a vector resolves execution order for free. State has no order problem; its problem is identity and sharing, which tuples with entity references were built for. Frames and the operand stack, which are sequences, stay positional inside the state, exactly as instructions stay positional inside a segment.\"]\n   304\t          [:h3 \"The Boundary: Tuples Travel, Execution Is Native\"]\n   305\t          [:p\n   306\t           \"The last decision fixes what the tuple set is for. It is the \"\n   307\t           [:em \"transport form only\"]\n   308\t           \". It exists between a lift and a lower. At rest in the tuple space it is queryable by the ordinary query interpreter, so \\\"which parked continuations close over this stream\\\" is a Datalog question rather than a decoder walk, and that is a real capability. But nothing executes it. On arrival the destination VM lowers the tuple set into whatever representation makes execution native there: yin.vm's image, a Wasm linear memory, a Dart isolate's heap. The hot loop runs in that native representation and never queries the transport relation; transients are one way to build it, not a requirement. An earlier design note on the CESK machine in the tuple space proposed, speculatively, that the transition function itself be a Datalog query over the index, and later in the same note proposed the persistence-boundary model, where state is flushed only on park, migrate, or checkpoint. This decision supersedes the first proposal and follows the second. The queryable form is the one at rest.\"]\n   309\t          [:p\n   310\t           \"In the geometry of Part Two this is the cleanest statement yet. The native representation is the fiber over each point. The tuple set is the point-independent representative that transport actually carries. The connection is the pair of maps between them. A foreign VM inherits the whole UCF contract as it stands: the envelope, the dependency closure, the pending waits and their dynamic reasons, the scheduler and fresh-name state, referenced parked records and module stores, profile satisfaction, total refusal of malformed input, and preservation of the next observable step and the effect trace. What it owes \"\n   311\t           [:em \"in addition\"]\n   312\t           \", for its own representation, is three things:\"]\n   313\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   314\t           [:li\n   315\t            [:strong \"Lift.\"]\n   316\t            \" At a safepoint, project native state to the tuple set, or refuse naming the entity and attribute. Content entities hash; identity entities carry tempids; outward references become names under a profile, stream descriptors, or refusals.\"]\n   317\t           [:li\n   318\t            [:strong \"Lower.\"]\n   319\t            \" Load a tuple set into native state through the destination's own loader, into an isolated task, with identity entities remapped to fresh native ids; or answer profile mismatch, or non-portable for this engine.\"]\n   320\t           [:li\n   321\t            [:strong \"Parity.\"]\n   322\t            \" Two laws, stated separately because they are different kinds of equality. Canonical round trip: lift after lower of a canonical tuple set gives it back, the direction the specification already states for foreign-engine frames, with identity-bearing sets compared under an explicit alias-preserving renaming of ids. Native observational equivalence: lower after lift on one engine need not reproduce the literal native state, since fresh ids and seals differ, but it must preserve the next observable step, the pending waits, the result, and the effect trace. Then the cross-engine row: running the corpus to each safepoint on engine B yields the same canonical tuple set the reference machine yields. That row is the one that catches a VM whose integers overflow or whose \"\n   323\t            [:code \"+\"]\n   324\t            \" disagrees; format cannot supply it, and the profile check only narrows which bindings may be tried.\"]]\n   325\t          [:p\n   326\t           \"Two obligations remain outside the value question and should not be folded into it. Reconstruction: a compiling engine must keep enough at every safepoint to rebuild the environment, operand stack, and frames, which is deoptimization metadata in the JIT sense, and it is what makes lift total or partial for that engine. Full coverage, every canonical safepoint a native safepoint with total reconstruction there, is not a new demand: the specification's foreign-engine contract already requires identical canonical safepoint sets, effect-producing calls included. It is the right demand for a compiled backend generated from the canonical vector, and it is scoped to such programs; it does not claim that an arbitrary Dart isolate or Wasm runtime stack is inspectable or resumable, and a destination that cannot meet a required capability refuses, which is hygiene working, not failing. And effects: a native engine's interaction with its host goes through stream effects like any other, and a protected commitment inherits the complete custody contract of Part Two, the fenced writer with occurrence, epoch, and operation id, the enrolled consumer, atomic admission, intent comparison, durable dedup, and replay or fail-stop. Fencing remains opt-in per stream, and fork compositions remain valid. The custody layer does not move at all, because it lives in the tuple space above every engine and never asks which one holds the grant.\"]\n   327\t          [:h3 \"Why This Is the Hygiene Move Again\"]\n   328\t          [:p\n   329\t           \"The path from the first proposal to the decision is the same lesson Part One drew, learned a third time. The AST-level proposal was a renaming scheme: give every reference a second name in a different coordinate system. Hygiene does not work by adding names; it works by making each reference carry the context that gives it meaning, and the canonical vector already does that. The CBOR proposal was the textual macro's mistake: treat the serialized form as the meaning. The tuple decision is syntax objects for state: every entity is a value plus the mark that says whether it is content or identity, and every outward reference is a name plus the profile that binds it. Changing the carrier from nested maps to tuples does not change what must be carried; it only removes the cases where the old carrier had to say the same thing three different ways.\"]\n   330\t          [:p\n   331\t           \"Status, so the essay does not get ahead of the specification: the decisions in this part are design, not landed code. The nested value grammar is what the current implementation ships and tests. The tuple form of the transported state, its schema, and the conformance harness for a second engine are the next specification change, and they reopen sections with tests behind them.\"]\n   332\t          [:h2 \"The Lineage\"]\n   333\t          [:p\n   334\t           \"None of this is new; all of it is newly joined. Plan 9 taught that a resource is a name in a namespace and that the namespace is per process, so the same path can mean different files to different processes without conflict. UCF's isolated store is that lesson: the resumed task gets its own namespace, and no ambient binding leaks in. Datomic taught that a fact is immutable and that identity is a value computed over content, so two writers who derive the same fact converge. UCF's \"\n   335\t           [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n   336\t           \" is that lesson, with the one necessary refinement that content identity and occurrence identity must be kept apart. Lisp taught, through forty years of macro pain, that a name is not a meaning until it carries its binding context. UCF's primitive markers, contract stamps, and content hashes are that lesson applied to a runtime instead of a reader. Just-in-time compilers taught that compiled code can be abandoned mid-flight and the interpreter's frame rebuilt from metadata kept at safepoints, and that the reverse, on-stack replacement, enters compiled code at those same points. Part Three's lift is deoptimization across a machine boundary, and its lower is analogous to on-stack replacement, though it may simply start a fresh native task from the canonical frame rather than enter a running activation; the tuple set is the interpreter frame they meet at.\"]\n   337\t          [:p\n   338\t           \"And geometry taught two things. Differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with. Sheaf theory taught that local pieces, each correct on its own patch, do not add up to a global object unless a gluing condition says how they agree on the overlaps, and that the condition is extra structure which nothing in the pieces supplies. UCF's seventh section is the second lesson, written in datoms: the connection is Parts One and Three, and the ledger is the gluing.\"]\n   339\t          [:p\n   340\t           \"A continuation is a value, not a session. That is the stance at the head of the UCF specification, and everything else follows from taking it seriously. A value can be content-addressed, so it can be deduplicated and verified. A value can be carried by anything, so no carrier is privileged. A value can be read by anyone, so custody must be arbitrated above the carrier by a boundary that actually possesses something. Hygiene makes the value mean the same thing everywhere. The lease makes sure it runs in only one place at a time. Between them, a suspended computation becomes a vector in a bundle over the distributed world, and UCF is the connection that lets it travel.\"]\n   341\t          [:p [:strong \"Learn more:\"]]\n   342\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   343\t           [:li\n   344\t            [:a\n   345\t             {:href \"/blog/continuations-universal-semantic-kernel.blog\"}\n   346\t             \"Why Continuations Are the Universal Semantic Kernel\"]\n   347\t            \" (why the CESK configuration is the right object to transport in the first place)\"]\n   348\t           [:li\n   349\t            [:a\n   350\t             {:href \"/blog/computation-moves-data-stays.blog\"}\n   351\t             \"Computation Moves, Data Stays\"]\n   352\t            \" (the architectural stance that makes migration the default rather than the exception)\"]\n   353\t           [:li\n   354\t            [:a\n   355\t             {:href \"/blog/beyond-ffi-yin-vm-datom-streaming.blog\"}\n   356\t             \"Beyond FFI: Yin.vm and Datom Streaming\"]\n   357\t            \" (how streams carry opaque values, and why that is the carrier role)\"]\n   358\t           [:li\n   359\t            [:a {:href \"/dao-space.chp\"} \"DaoSpace\"]\n   360\t            \" (the tuple space where the arbitration ledger lives)\"]\n   361\t           [:li\n   362\t            [:a {:href \"/yin.chp\"} \"Yin VM Documentation\"]\n   363\t            \" (the canonical CESK and the semantic contract that UCF stamps)\"]]]]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog | sed -n '125,247p'; rg -n 'epoch|tenure|live lease|admission|stale|re-grant|quarantin|expired|completed' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | tail -95; sed -n '2037,2130p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2245,2376p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog | sed -n '125,247p'; rg -n 'epoch|tenure|live lease|admission|stale|re-grant|quarantin|expired|completed' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | tail -95; sed -n '2037,2130p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '2245,2376p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\"","aggregated_output":"   125\t          [:h2 \"Part Two: The Geometric Parallel\"]\n   126\t          [:h3 \"Moving a Vector on a Curved Surface\"]\n   127\t          [:p\n   128\t           \"In differential geometry, a vector lives in the tangent space at a point. It does not exist \\\"everywhere\\\"; it exists \"\n   129\t           [:em \"at\"]\n   130\t           \" a point. To compare a vector at one point with a vector at another, you must transport one of them along a path. On a flat surface this is trivial: slide it and keep it parallel to itself. On a curved surface there is no canonical way to keep a vector \\\"the same\\\" as it moves, because the tangent spaces at different points are different vector spaces with no built-in identification.\"]\n   131\t          [:p\n   132\t           \"The structure that supplies the identification is a \"\n   133\t           [:strong \"connection\"]\n   134\t           \", written \"\n   135\t           [:code \"∇\"]\n   136\t           \". A connection says, for each infinitesimal step along a path, how the tangent space at the new point is identified with the one just left. Parallel transport along a curve \"\n   137\t           [:code \"γ\"]\n   138\t           \" is the rule \"\n   139\t           [:code \"∇_γ̇ v = 0\"]\n   140\t           \": the covariant derivative of the vector along the curve vanishes. The vector changes coordinates as it moves, because the coordinates at each point differ, but its intrinsic content is preserved by the connection.\"]\n   141\t          [:p\n   142\t           \"The general picture is a \"\n   143\t           [:strong \"fiber bundle\"]\n   144\t           \". There is a base manifold \"\n   145\t           [:code \"M\"]\n   146\t           \" of points. Over each point \"\n   147\t           [:code \"x\"]\n   148\t           \" sits a fiber \"\n   149\t           [:code \"F_x\"]\n   150\t           \", a copy of some space of states. The total space is all fibers glued together. A section picks one state per point. A connection says how to carry a state from one fiber to a neighboring fiber. And the deep fact is this: carrying a state around a closed loop and returning to the start does not in general give back the same state. The failure to return is \"\n   151\t           [:strong \"holonomy\"]\n   152\t           \", and its infinitesimal measure is \"\n   153\t           [:strong \"curvature\"]\n   154\t           \". Curvature is the obstruction to a globally consistent notion of \\\"the same state everywhere.\\\" Hold on to the word \"\n   155\t           [:em \"section\"]\n   156\t           \", because it is the one that will matter. Curvature is what goes wrong when the connection itself is path-dependent; there is a different and simpler way for \\\"the same state everywhere\\\" to fail, when a flat connection hands several readers one state each and nothing says which of the resulting sections is the real one. UCF's problem turns out to be the second kind.\"]\n   157\t          [:h3 \"The Bundle of Execution\"]\n   158\t          [:p \"Every term of that picture has a referent in datom.world, and the correspondence is exact enough to compute with.\"]\n   159\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   160\t           [:li\n   161\t            [:strong \"Base manifold M: distributed spacetime.\"]\n   162\t            \" The points are places a computation can be: this JVM, that Node.js process, a Dart isolate on a phone, a checkpoint persisted at a particular transaction time. Points are separated both in space (different hosts) and in time (a park at t₁, a resume at t₂). The manifold is not flat: hosts differ in codec, in number representation, in which primitives exist, in which code is already loaded.\"]\n   163\t           [:li\n   164\t            [:strong \"Fiber F_x: the local CESK state space.\"]\n   165\t            \" Over each point sits the space of configurations \"\n   166\t            [:code \"⟨C, E, S, K⟩\"]\n   167\t            \" that machine can hold. Control, environment, store, continuation. These fibers are isomorphic in the abstract (every conforming engine lowers into the canonical CESK), but they are not \"\n   168\t            [:em \"identical\"]\n   169\t            \": each has its own local ids, its own store keys, its own resource table, its own host representations. Exactly as tangent spaces at different points are each ℝⁿ yet have no canonical identification, two VMs each hold CESK configurations yet have no canonical map between their segment ids.\"]\n   170\t           [:li\n   171\t            [:strong \"Path γ: the dao.stream.\"]\n   172\t            \" The curve along which a suspended computation moves is a stream. It may be a file, a socket, a DHT entry, an append log. The specification calls this role the \"\n   173\t            [:em \"carrier\"]\n   174\t            \" and says of it: sees opaque data, holds nothing. That is exactly what a path in the base is. A path does not carry meaning. It carries position.\"]\n   175\t           [:li\n   176\t            [:strong \"Connection ∇: the Universal Continuation Format.\"]\n   177\t            \" UCF is the rule that identifies a configuration in the fiber over the emitter with a configuration in the fiber over the resumer. The lift takes a local configuration to a point-independent representative: code by content hash, primitives by name plus profile, store as an isolated slice, markers in a disjoint grammar. The lower takes that representative back into the resumer's local coordinates: fresh local ids minted, resources remapped into a private table, references re-sealed under the receiver's own capability secret. Content is invariant; coordinates change. That is \"\n   178\t            [:code \"∇_γ̇ v = 0\"]\n   179\t            \" for execution state.\"]]\n   180\t          [:p\n   181\t           \"A connection has one defining property beyond preserving content: it is defined pointwise along the path and depends on nothing global. UCF has the same property, and it is a stated invariant rather than an accident. The reference engine is \"\n   182\t           [:code \"yin.vm.semantic\"]\n   183\t           \", and a foreign engine conforms by lifting into and lowering from its canonical configuration, never by extending it. There is no global coordinate system. There is only the local lift at the emitter, the local lower at the resumer, and the connection that relates them.\"]\n   184\t          [:h3 \"Where Transport Stops Being Enough\"]\n   185\t          [:p\n   186\t           \"If every checkpoint were read exactly once, transport would be the end of the story. Lift, carry, lower, done. The reason UCF has a seventh section, longer than all the others, is that nothing in the carrier guarantees a single reading, and the failure has a name: \"\n   187\t           [:strong \"the resumption race\"]\n   188\t           \".\"]\n   189\t          [:p\n   190\t           \"The emitter parks, lifts, appends the value to a medium, and keeps its local parked record. Three readers see the value on the stream. The emitter's own wait set wakes because the stream it was waiting on delivered. Now four machines believe they may continue one computation. Nothing in dao.stream can stop them: a stream has no privileged reader and no destructive read.\"]\n   191\t          [:p\n   192\t           \"It is tempting to call this curvature, and the first draft of this essay did: carry the state around a loop and it comes back different. But that is not what happens. The connection is flat. Lift and lower are deterministic and do not depend on the path, and every one of the four machines holds a faithful copy of the same value. Nothing was distorted in transit. What went wrong is that the value was \"\n   193\t           [:em \"copied\"]\n   194\t           \", because a stream read does not consume, and now there are four candidate futures over one point in the computation's history where there must be one. In the language of sheaves this is a gluing problem, not a transport problem. Each reader holds a local section, a perfectly good continuation over its own neighbourhood. The question is which local sections may be glued into the one global section that is the computation's world line, and a connection has no opinion on that. Something else has to supply the compatibility condition.\"]\n   195\t          [:p\n   196\t           \"UCF does not pretend the readers will sort it out among themselves. It supplies the gluing condition, and makes it the only one:\"]\n   197\t          [:ul.bulleted\n   198\t           [:li\n   199\t            [:strong \"Content is not occurrence.\"]\n   200\t            \" The same parked computation may be encoded more than once with different sharing thresholds, so the encodings have different content addresses while remaining one checkpoint occurrence. \"\n   201\t            [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n   202\t            \" is content identity, for deduplication and materialization. \"\n   203\t            [:code \":yin.k/occurrence\"]\n   204\t            \" is the thing custody names, minted exactly once when a park is first exported, carried unchanged by every snapshot variant and every retry. In geometric terms: the vector has many coordinate expressions, but one identity in the bundle, and the occurrence is the point over which the candidate sections must be glued.\"]\n   205\t           [:li\n   206\t            [:strong \"Resumption is a take, a take is a write, exclusion is a lease.\"]\n   207\t            \" Custody is arbitrated above the stream on an arbitration medium, a dao.space whose facts are ordinary datoms, using the dao.lease vocabulary unchanged. The leased subject is the occurrence. Only grantor-authored facts establish terms. The grantor is the transactor of the occurrence's execution ledger, the one boundary that actually possesses the right to admit effects as this occurrence.\"]\n   208\t           [:li\n   209\t            [:strong \"Epochs are the fence.\"]\n   210\t            \" The ledger carries an epoch datom for each occurrence, advanced by the grantor on every reclaim. A resumed incarnation emits through a fenced writer that stamps each effect with its lease, its epoch, and a stable operation id \"\n   211\t            [:code \"{:yin.k/occurrence O :yin.k/seq n}\"]\n   212\t            \". An enrolled consumer checks the epoch, compares the operation's canonical intent against its durable dedup record, and commits the side effect, all in one atomic boundary. A stale epoch is refused at commitment. A lapsed holder is never told to stop; it stops at its own lease bound, and what it did past the bound is distinguishable after the fact by epoch and op-id.\"]\n   213\t           [:li\n   214\t            [:strong \"The absence of a lapse is never evidence of tenure.\"]\n   215\t            \" The first draft tried to fence by having consumers look for \"\n   216\t            [:code \":lapsed\"]\n   217\t            \" records and drop effects whose incarnation had one. That is correlation, not fencing: a partitioned consumer cannot see the lapse, and accepts stale effects. The epoch check at commitment replaces it. In the geometric reading, two local sections do not glue because nobody has yet reported that they conflict; they glue because a compatibility condition says so, and the condition must be checkable by the one who commits. The epoch is that condition, issued by the authority and carried on every effect: not a measurement of anything, but the term under which a candidate is admitted as the continuation.\"]\n   218\t           [:li\n   219\t            [:strong \"Causal linearity is a chain of edges.\"]\n   220\t            \" Completion of a checkpoint is not the holder's report; it is the grantor's ledger transition that atomically records the lapse, the occurrence-completed fact, and exactly one successor or terminal edge. A successor published without an accepted report is an orphan, queryable as history but never a grant. The world line of a computation is thereby a single path through occurrences, and the ledger is what makes it single.\"]]\n   221\t          [:p\n   222\t           \"Notice what UCF does \"\n   223\t           [:em \"not\"]\n   224\t           \" do. It does not make the carrier consume what it delivers, and it does not pretend that reading is taking. It does not promise exactly-once effects through a bare stream; a composition that needs them must wire a fenced writer and an enrolled consumer, and one that cannot reach the authority must suspend protected commitment or declare at-least-once. A composition that cannot wire a dao.space transactor as grantor cannot offer exclusive custody at all; it composes fork semantics only. The specification calls this an honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over. Geometry agrees: local sections do not glue themselves. Without a gluing condition you have a family of candidates, not a global section, and no choice of coordinates will turn one into the other.\"]\n   225\t          [:h2 \"Why the Two Parallels Are One\"]\n   226\t          [:p\n   227\t           \"Hygiene and parallel transport look like different ideas from different fields. They are the same idea seen at two scales.\"]\n   228\t          [:p\n   229\t           \"Hygiene is the \"\n   230\t           [:em \"fiberwise\"]\n   231\t           \" story. It says: a value in a fiber must carry enough context that the lower, in a different fiber, resolves every reference against the origin rather than the destination. Content-addressed code, stamped contracts, isolated stores, disjoint tagged markers, named primitives. These are the marks and scopes of the syntax object, placed on execution state. They make the connection \"\n   232\t           [:strong \"well defined\"]\n   233\t           \": given a lifted value, there is exactly one configuration it lowers to on any conforming engine.\"]\n   234\t          [:p\n   235\t           \"Parallel transport is the \"\n   236\t           [:em \"global\"]\n   237\t           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined, perfectly flat connection gives you one faithful copy per reader, and when the carrier does not consume, that is several candidate sections over one point with nothing to choose between them. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They are the gluing condition that makes the world line \"\n   238\t           [:strong \"single\"]\n   239\t           \": of all the candidate continuations over an occurrence, exactly one is admitted as the computation, and every other is distinguishable afterwards as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n   240\t          [:p\n   241\t           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree: one expansion per site, nothing to glue. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and partitions, retries, crash restarts, and redelivery multiply the readers. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n   242\t          [:h2 \"Part Three: Transport to a Different Machine\"]\n   243\t          [:p\n   244\t           \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways. One qualifier governs everything below: these are design decisions, not landed code. What ships today is the nested value grammar of Part One; the tuple form described here is the next specification change.\"]\n   245\t          [:h3 \"What the Specification Already Says\"]\n   246\t          [:p\n   247\t           \"UCF has one sentence on the subject, and it turns out to be the whole rule: \"\n2161:  `:yin.k/epoch` and `:yin.k/op-id` could be read, as claims. They\n2164:- The map carries neither `:yin.k/admission` nor `:yin.k/status`. A\n2176:**The grant epoch binding.** The grantor publishes one more custody\n2184: :yin.k/epoch      e}\n2197:  confers no fenced tenure.\n2216:- **How a reader learns the epoch.** Only from a valid binding. A\n2219:  state inside its admission transition. An epoch a holder\n2222:- **The counter.** Each occurrence has one epoch in its ledger\n2227:  epoch current at that grant, so the first grant binds 0 and the\n2230:- (M-next C, slice C6.) **The epoch change** is the grantor's fact\n2236:   :yin.k/epoch      e}             ; the epoch after the reclaim\n2242:  bound it is still written, carrying the unchanged epoch.\n2260:- **Restart.** The epoch never decreases, is never reset, and is\n2261:  never reused. A grantor that restarts recovers the epoch from its\n2263:  (7.7.7). A grantor that cannot recover the epoch grants nothing\n2265:  epoch survived. A new authority never starts an old occurrence at\n2266:  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\n2273:  the tenure, leaves the epoch unchanged, and permanently exhausts\n2274:  the occurrence. Thereafter admission returns `:suspended` before\n2275:  tenure checking, and no effect commits. Exhaustion is derived\n2277:  An exhausted tenure cannot produce an eligible exclusive\n2281:  In the ledger, that lapse's epoch change carries the lease's own\n2282:  binding epoch, which only a lease bound at 2^52-1 can do; that is\n2286:  tenure from what it observes: `:dao.lease/lapsed` does not cross\n2288:  partial. The binding publishes the grant's authoritative epoch.\n2291:by one atomic transition against the authority's admission resource\n2299:   quarantined after an intent conflict (below) answers the same.\n2304:   and `e` equals its current epoch, else `:stale`. Staleness is\n2305:   decided before the dedup record is consulted: a stale envelope is\n2317:   admission answers `:suspended` without changing tenure,\n2318:   quarantine, or dedup state. Unavailable evidence is not evidence\n2319:   of an invalid id, and this suspension comes after the tenure\n2320:   check, so a stale holder is still answered `:stale`. The\n2343:admission boundary itself establishes it. An outcome that leaves the\n2348:the fenced admission boundary. The authority may already hold a\n2361:variant admission. (M-next C, slice C5.) The authority admits an\n2373:per arbitration admission resource, across every enrolled target that\n2399:occurrence's live lease. Otherwise it is refused `:orphan` unless it\n2409:established, in an authenticated admission, quarantines the\n2411:admissions answer `:suspended`. Recovery or compensation is a\n2413:of a conflict quarantines nothing. (M-next C, slice C8.) A\n2414:quarantined occurrence cannot complete: a report is refused, and a\n2415:release or policy lapse records only the lapse and its epoch change.\n2429:duplicates an edge nor advances an already-reclaimed lease's epoch again.\n2538:- A grant observed without a valid epoch binding is\n2542:**Amendment r5: admission outcomes.** The outcome of an enrolled\n2543:consumer's admission (7.7.8) is data that the consumer appends to the\n2545:dispatching on a second key, `:yin.k/admission`. It is not a\n2555:| `:yin.k/admission` | Meaning | Also carries |\n2559:| `:stale` | tenure is not current | `:yin.k/observed-epoch` |\n2569:  the enrolled consumer or admission authority for that target. An\n2576:- `:stale` carries `:yin.k/observed-epoch`, the occurrence's current\n2577:  epoch; `:yin.k/observed-lease`, its active lease, when one exists;\n2589:  (7.2.1). It also answers for an exhausted or quarantined\n2593:- A defective envelope has no admission outcome; its structured\n2599:  `:stale` and `:intent-conflict` leave the wait undischarged and\n2602:  occurrence is quarantined (7.7.8).\n2607:  serves the `:committed` outcome of every fenced admission its\n2629:`:tenure-ended`, `:tenure-unconfirmed` and\n2636:protocol, never the admission outcome `:intent-conflict`: an input\n2638:and quarantines nothing (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.2). The two\n2665:state closure, custody enforcement, and effect admission had not earned.\n2675:  transactor as grantor, the occurrence ledger with epochs, and the\n2692:- **Enforceable fencing.** Epoch-checked, atomic-with-commitment admission\n2753:live occurrence/lease/epoch, and fresh `lease/holding?` before activation,\n2756:per-effect tenure guards remain in force, including across computation and\n2759:uses fresh evidence without refreshing the original tenure basis; authenticated\n2785:tenure checks. A delayed reply cannot revive a run past its local bound.\n2797:when present, `:yin.k/binding` naming the local occurrence, lease, and epoch.\n2809:re-establish tenure.\n2965:  enrolled protected effect commits at most once across re-grants.\n2970:  records. Inject publication failure, retry, stale lease, consumer\n2972:  release. After a crash, evict a kept-cursor value so the re-granted run\n2978:  `:yin.k/awaiting-grant`. Reclaim advances the epoch; stale effects fail\n2979:  at commitment. At the enrolled consumer, epoch check, operation-intent\n2983:  A re-grant either replays durable inputs or fails closed on divergence.\n2985:  protected consumer suspends admission. Without enrollment, the stream\n2989:  publishes. Exclusive custody, epoch admission, crash recovery, and the\n3035:- Invariant: A version-1 body, envelope, binding, and admission\n3101:   diagnostic with its defect and provenance and no admission\n3108:   the epoch by one in the lapse's transaction; a successor starts\n3111:   float epoch establishes nothing. Reopen the ledger and assert\n3112:   the epoch is recovered and raised, never reset. Stage: C for the\n3117:   in order: unreadable authority, wrong author, stale epoch, stale\n3122:   quarantines the occurrence. Add forged outcomes: a `:committed`\n3124:   nothing and quarantine nothing. Add an unknown-effect transport\n3129:   `:suspended` with tenure, quarantine, and dedup state unchanged,\n3130:   and a stale holder still answers `:stale`. Add a snapshot\n3133:   outcome map of 7.9 for each and that stale wins over an existing\n3136:   export. An epoch at 2^52-1 is tested twice: while valid, its\n3139:   no successor is eligible, and admission answers `:suspended`\n3140:   before tenure. The values 2^52 and -1 are refused on the bytes.\n3141:   Stage: C for the epoch, D for the sequence.\n3160:exclusive custody itself, atomic admission, crash recovery, input\n3162:recovery, after quarantine, exhaustion, or lost authority state,\n### 7.7.8 Fenced custody, version 1: sequence, envelope, epoch binding\n\n(Amendment r5, 2026-10-04; `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.3 item 2.) This\nsection publishes the grammar that 7.7.5 argues for. The reasons stay\nin 7.7.5; the runtime, ledger, and step contract stay in\n`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2. Nothing here adds a key to the lease\nvocabulary or to a DaoStream outcome map. Every rule applies to\nversion-1 bodies only (7.2.1).\n\n**Exact integers.** `:yin.k/epoch`, `:yin.k/seq` and\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq` are nonnegative exact integers no greater than\n2^52-1 (4503599627370495), the bound within which every host compares\nand increments exactly (`dao.lease.md`, *Keys*, uses the same one).\nThe kind is the canonical codec's integer kind: a float is not an\ninteger even when it is integral. A value outside the range or of\nanother kind makes its carrier defective: a body is\n`:yin.k/undecodable`, an envelope or a binding establishes nothing.\nNo counter wraps, saturates silently, or restarts.\n\n**Operation sequence state.** A task under a grant holds one counter,\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, on its root. Install children draw from it.\n\n1. *Assign.* Before the first attempt of a write to an enrolled\n   stream, the fenced writer takes `n`, the counter's value, forms\n   the id `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` with `P` the\n   occurrence its grant names, stores the id on the wait entry, and\n   sets the counter to `n+1`. The three happen as one step over the\n   machine value, before any append.\n2. *Retain.* The id stays on the entry while the write is unfinished:\n   a `full` path, an append of unknown effect, an acceptance with no\n   admission outcome yet, or a `:suspended` outcome. A retry reuses\n   the id and never takes another.\n3. *Carry.* At a park the id travels in the pending (7.4.3) and the\n   counter in the header (7.2.1). The successor's counter is the\n   holder's counter at that park, so the sequence continues along\n   the chain and never restarts at a new occurrence.\n4. *Restore.* Lower sets the fresh task's counter to the carried\n   value exactly. There is no local value to take a maximum with:\n   the receiving task is new (contrast `:yin.k/id-counter`, 7.6.3).\n5. *Regrant.* A holder granted the same checkpoint again starts from\n   the same bytes, so from the same counter and the same carried\n   ids. Re-executed writes take the same sequence numbers in the\n   same order as long as its inputs replay; where they do not, the\n   intent comparison below catches the divergence.\n6. *Exhaust.* The largest assignable sequence is 2^52-2. A counter\n   equal to 2^52-1 is exhausted: the writer assigns nothing and\n   appends nothing, the write stays an undischarged wait, and a lift\n   of that task refuses `:yin.k/non-portable`, kind\n   `:op-seq-exhausted`. Recovery is outside this protocol.\n\n**The fenced envelope.** A fenced writer wraps each value it appends\ntoward an enrolled consumer:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/envelope    :yin.k/fenced-v1   ; dispatch key; closed\n :yin.k/incarnation L                  ; the writer's :dao.lease/lease\n :yin.k/epoch       e                  ; from L's grant binding\n :yin.k/op-id       {:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}\n :yin.k/value       v}                 ; the program value, verbatim\n```\n\n- All five keys are required. `:yin.k/op-id` has exactly its two\n  keys. A consumer ignores other qualified keys, and they are never\n  part of intent. An envelope that fails these rules, or dispatches\n  on a value other than `:yin.k/fenced-v1`, is defective (below).\n- **Inside program data: only `:yin.k/value`.** It is the value the\n  program wrote, unchanged and uninterpreted. A program value that\n  looks like an envelope is payload: it is wrapped like any other\n  and never read as one.\n- **Outside program data: everything else.** The envelope exists\n  between the fenced writer and the enrolled boundary and nowhere\n  else. It is never in a frame, an environment, a store, or a body;\n  a pending carries the retained value and the id beside it, and\n  the writer rebuilds the envelope at each retry from the id and\n  its current binding. What the consumer's commit makes visible\n  downstream is the consumer's contract; UCF requires only that the\n  payload it commits is `:yin.k/value`.\n- The envelope names no target, no effect kind, and no intent. The\n  consumer takes the target identity and the effect kind from the\n  boundary the envelope arrived at, and computes intent itself.\n- The envelope carries no credential. The holder is authenticated\n  by the attribution the composition supplies (`dao.lease.md`,\n  *Composition duties*). An envelope whose attributed author is not\n  the holder that the binding of `L` names is a fact about that\n  author: it is defective.\n\n**Defective envelopes.** A defective envelope commits no effect and\ncreates no dedup record. It yields no admission outcome: an outcome\nwould be attributed to the holder its fields claim, and those fields\nare exactly what cannot be trusted. The consumer instead appends one\nstructured diagnostic to the diagnostic stream the composition\nsupplies:\n\n```clojure\n  `dao.lease` (it names no proposer), and the grantor's\n\n  ```clojure\n  {:yin.k/custody      :yin.k/refused\n   :yin.k/proposer     P\n   :dao.lease/proposal pid}\n  ```\n\n  A lease judge switches on the lease keys and ignores the refused\n  fact; a reader learns which proposal a refusal answered, keyed\n  `[proposer proposal-id]`, from the refused fact alone. The ledger\n  refuses either half\n  without the other. A proposal id is answered at most once, so a\n  candidate refused, or answered `:yin.k/not-holder` (7.8), proposes\n  again with a fresh proposal id.\n- **Restart.** The epoch never decreases, is never reset, and is\n  never reused. A grantor that restarts recovers the epoch from its\n  durable ledger and reclaims, which raises it, before it grants\n  (7.7.7). A grantor that cannot recover the epoch grants nothing\n  for that occurrence: 7.7.7's reclaim-and-regrant presumes the\n  epoch survived. A new authority never starts an old occurrence at\n  0. Recovery from lost epoch or dedup state is governance, outside\n  this protocol (7.7.5). (M-next C, slice C6.) Reopen runs 7.7.7's\n  steps, rebuilding the judge after the reclaims: a reclaim that does\n  not commit refuses the open, and reopen is retried.\n- **Exhaustion.** Epoch 2^52-1 is usable until a reclaim would\n  increment it: a grant bound at that value is valid, and its\n  holder's effects are admitted like any other's. That reclaim ends\n  the tenure, leaves the epoch unchanged, and permanently exhausts\n  the occurrence. Thereafter admission returns `:suspended` before\n  tenure checking, and no effect commits. Exhaustion is derived\n  from the ledger: a lapse recorded for the lease bound at 2^52-1.\n  An exhausted tenure cannot produce an eligible exclusive\n  successor: the authority accepts no completion and grants nothing\n  for the occurrence. Carrier bytes may remain as history, but a\n  publication cannot reopen custody. (M-next C, slices C6 and C8.)\n  In the ledger, that lapse's epoch change carries the lease's own\n  binding epoch, which only a lease bound at 2^52-1 can do; that is\n  the exhaustion. A report recorded at the bound is evidence: its\n  release exhausts the occurrence without completing it.\n- **Why a binding.** A remote holder cannot infer authoritative\n  tenure from what it observes: `:dao.lease/lapsed` does not cross\n  to it (`dao.lease.md`, *Carriage*), and its view of the ledger is\n  partial. The binding publishes the grant's authoritative epoch.\n\n**Admission.** An enrolled consumer admits each well-formed envelope\nby one atomic transition against the authority's admission resource\n(7.7.5). The checks run in this order and the first to fail decides\nthe outcome (7.9):\n\n1. *Authority.* The authority the consumer is enrolled with must be\n   readable. If it is unreachable, if its ledger for the occurrence\n   that `L` is bound to is not recoverable, or if that occurrence\n   is exhausted, the outcome is `:suspended`. An occurrence\n   quarantined after an intent conflict (below) answers the same.\n2. *Binding.* `L` has a valid binding, and the envelope's attributed\n   author is the holder it names. Otherwise the envelope is\n   defective: a diagnostic, and no outcome.\n3. *Tenure.* The bound occurrence is open, `L` is its active lease,\n   and `e` equals its current epoch, else `:stale`. Staleness is\n   decided before the dedup record is consulted: a stale envelope is\n   refused even when a record for its id exists.\n4. *Scope.* An operation assigned during the current occurrence\n   names that occurrence. An inherited operation must occur among\n   the retained pending operations of the authoritative checkpoint\n   granted to the holder, including its install children, and its\n   occurrence must be an ancestor through authoritative completion\n   records. Membership is derived from the accepted checkpoint, the\n   body the authority admitted for the granted occurrence; no\n   ancestry index and no pending-id index is stored. An id that\n   fails is defective, `:foreign-op-id`. For an inherited id, if\n   the authoritative checkpoint cannot be read and verified,\n   admission answers `:suspended` without changing tenure,\n   quarantine, or dedup state. Unavailable evidence is not evidence\n   of an invalid id, and this suspension comes after the tenure\n   check, so a stale holder is still answered `:stale`. The\n   checkpoint is selected by the authority's accepted record, never\n   by an envelope-supplied location. Current-occurrence ids require\n   no inherited-membership lookup. Keeping the accepted checkpoint\n   readable is a durability duty of the composition. (M-next C,\n   slice C9.) Ancestry is checked first, from the completion records\n   alone, before the checkpoint is read. A granted occurrence's\n   ancestors never change, so a non-ancestor is `:foreign-op-id`\n   permanently; only membership waits on a store that can be\n   repaired. Any offered variant whose bytes verify to the recorded\n   baseline is the checkpoint, because the variants share one\n   baseline. A store that is absent, closed or throws is unreadable.\n5. *Dedup.* With a record for the id: equal intent answers\n   `:replayed` with the recorded result and commits nothing;\n   different intent answers `:intent-conflict` and commits nothing.\n   With no record: the effect, its result, and the record\n   `{op-id -> {intent result}}` commit together, and the outcome is\n   `:committed`.\n\n**Results and uncertainty.** The recorded result is the effect's\ndefinitive outcome, as plain data; for a stream append it is the\ntarget's DaoStream outcome map, unchanged. A terminal refusal is a\nresult, recorded and replayed unchanged, but only when the atomic\nadmission boundary itself establishes it. An outcome that leaves the\neffect unknown, such as a `:dao.stream/transport-error` from a\ntransport whose failures are not declared clean, is not a result.\nUnknown transport acceptance establishes neither commitment nor\nabsence of commitment. The writer retains the id and retries through\nthe fenced admission boundary. The authority may already hold a\ncommitted result, which the retry must replay. An external effect\nwhose uncertainty cannot be reconciled within that atomic boundary\nis outside this guarantee. Backpressure, by contrast, is no\ncommitment: it is the writer path's own `:dao.stream/full`, and\nnothing was appended.\n\n**Snapshot variants.** Snapshot variants of one occurrence must\npreserve its operation baseline: the root next-operation sequence\nand the mapping of retained operation ids to canonical intents,\nincluding child operations. Equal id sets alone are not enough: a\nvariant could keep an id and substitute its payload. The authority\nrefuses a conflicting variant; an unavailable comparison suspends\nvariant admission. (M-next C, slice C5.) The authority admits an\noffer in this order: inspect the bytes; under the authority lock,\ndecide purely; store the body only when the decision will commit;\ncommit. A refused, replayed or uninspectable offer stores nothing. A\nstore that fails answers `:suspended` and commits nothing; a body\nstored before a commit that then fails is a harmless orphan.\n(M-next C, slice C9.) These mechanics realize the suspension above:\nthe comparison's basis, the recorded baseline, is always in the open\nauthority's projection, so the only unavailable comparison is that\nfailing store.\n\n**One dedup namespace.** The dedup records form one logical namespace\nper arbitration admission resource, across every enrolled target that\ntakes part in the guarantee, keyed by the operation id alone. Target\nidentity is part of the intent, never of the key. So a replay that\ndiverges and sends an id to another target meets the first target's\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The recast fixes the copying-versus-holonomy distinction, but two custody claims remain too strong: it says exactly one candidate is admitted for an occurrence, although re-grants can admit successive holders, and it calls the epoch the complete compatibility condition. I also found the old “manifold is not flat” sentence still present; the new sheaf language needs an explicit analogy qualifier."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,124p' public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog; sed -n '1655,1700p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1972,2028p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,124p' public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog; sed -n '1655,1700p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; sed -n '1972,2028p' docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"#:blog{:title\n       \"Hygienic Execution State: The Universal Continuation Format as Parallel Transport\",\n       :date #inst \"2026-10-09T00:00:00.000-00:00\",\n       :abstract\n       [:p\n        \"What is the Universal Continuation Format really doing? Two older ideas answer the question from opposite directions. Hygienic macro systems taught Lisp how to move \"\n        [:em \"code\"]\n        \" across lexical boundaries without accidental capture. Differential geometry taught physics how to move a \"\n        [:em \"vector\"]\n        \" across a curved manifold without distortion. UCF is both at once: it is hygiene for suspended computation, and it is the connection that parallel-transports a CESK configuration across the distributed spacetime of nodes, runtimes, and checkpoints. Transport alone is not enough: a carrier that never consumes what it delivers hands the same checkpoint to every reader, and the lease is the gluing condition that makes one of those readings the computation. A third part extends the transport past the boundary it was designed for: from one yin.vm to a different virtual machine altogether. The extension turned out to be narrow, and the path to seeing that it is narrow is recorded here, wrong turns included.\"],\n       :content\n       [:section.blog-article\n        [:div.section-inner\n         [:article\n          :$blog-title\n          :$blog-date\n          [:h2 \"The Question\"]\n          [:p\n           \"A continuation in yin.vm is a value. A parked configuration is a record \"\n           [:code \"{segment pc env stack k}\"]\n           \" plus a reason and some resource ids. There is no handle in it, no closure object, no thread. On its own machine it is complete: the VM can pick it up and keep going.\"]\n          [:p\n           \"On any other machine it means nothing. The segment is a tempid minted by one transactor. The stream ids are keys into one VM's store. The environment may hold host functions. Nothing in the record says which primitives it assumed, which opcode table it was compiled against, or which version of \"\n           [:code \"resolve-var\"]\n           \" gave its symbols their meaning. The specification puts it bluntly: the record is \"\n           [:em \"locally complete and globally meaningless\"]\n           \".\"]\n          [:p\n           \"The Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the lift of that local record into a self-describing value that any conforming engine can lower back into its own registers, together with the discipline that governs who may lower it. That is the mechanical description. This essay is about what the mechanism \"\n           [:em \"is\"]\n           \", seen from two older disciplines that solved the same problem for different objects.\"]\n          [:h2 \"Part One: The Lexical Parallel\"]\n          [:h3 \"The Capture Problem\"]\n          [:p\n           \"Early Lisp macros were textual rewriters with a dangerous freedom. A macro expanded into code, and that code landed inside whatever lexical scope the call site happened to have. If the macro introduced a temporary named \"\n           [:code \"tmp\"]\n           \" and the caller already had a \"\n           [:code \"tmp\"]\n           \", the two collided. If the macro referred to \"\n           [:code \"list\"]\n           \" and the caller had shadowed \"\n           [:code \"list\"]\n           \" with a local, the macro silently called the wrong thing. The code fragment carried its text but not its meaning, and meaning was assigned by the host scope it fell into.\"]\n          [:p\n           \"This is \"\n           [:strong \"unhygienic expansion\"]\n           \": identifiers are bare names, and bare names resolve wherever they land.\"]\n          [:h3 \"What Hygiene Does\"]\n          [:p\n           \"Kohlbecker's algorithm, the Clinger and Rees explicit-renaming systems, and Dybvig's \"\n           [:code \"syntax-case\"]\n           \" all converge on one move. A syntax object is not a bare symbol. It is a symbol \"\n           [:em \"plus its lexical context\"]\n           \": a set of marks, scopes, or renaming environments that record where the identifier was born. When the fragment is transplanted into a new scope, the resolver consults the carried context, not the ambient one. An identifier introduced by the macro resolves in the macro's definition environment. An identifier supplied by the caller resolves in the caller's. The two cannot be confused because each carries its own origin.\"]\n          [:p\n           \"Hygiene is therefore not a restriction on what macros may do. It is a change in what an identifier \"\n           [:em \"is\"]\n           \". A name becomes a binding with provenance, and provenance travels with the fragment.\"]\n          [:h3 \"Unhygienic Continuation Transfer\"]\n          [:p\n           \"Now replace the code fragment with a suspended computation, and the lexical boundary with a machine boundary. Every capture failure of the macro world has an exact runtime twin:\"]\n          [:ul.bulleted\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Identifier capture becomes pointer capture.\"]\n            \" The record names \"\n            [:code \":segment 123\"]\n            \". On the receiving machine, \"\n            [:code \"123\"]\n            \" is different code or no code. A continuation that names code by tempid is not a value; it is a pointer into a heap that did not travel.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Shadowing becomes store pollution.\"]\n            \" Resolution in yin.vm runs environment, then store, then primitives. If the migrated slice were merged into the receiver's store, a receiver that happened to bind \"\n            [:code \"+\"]\n            \" would change what the migrated program computes, with no collision at all. The first UCF draft said in two places that the slice both wins on collision and merges under the local store. That contradiction was the shadowing bug, rediscovered.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Free variables become unportable handles.\"]\n            \" A host function in the environment, a live stream handle, the FFI pair: these are references into one process's address space. Carried naively, they are dangling pointers dressed as values.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Marker forgery becomes data ambiguity.\"]\n            \" The first draft encoded closures as ordinary maps like \"\n            [:code \"{:yin.k/primitive '+}\"]\n            \". But a program may legitimately contain that very map as data. A decoder could not tell the encoder's marker from the program's literal, exactly as an unhygienic expander cannot tell the macro's \"\n            [:code \"tmp\"]\n            \" from the caller's.\"]]\n          [:h3 \"Hygienic Execution State\"]\n          [:p\n           \"UCF's answer is the hygiene move, applied to every component of the configuration. Each answer attaches context so that the value resolves against its origin rather than against wherever it lands.\"]\n          [:ul.bulleted\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Code carries its identity, not its address.\"]\n            \" Every segment a continuation may reference bears \"\n            [:code \":yin.code/hash\"]\n            \", the content address of its canonical instruction vector: one positional tuple per instruction, pc as index, operands saturated with the loader's defaults, provenance and entity ids excluded. Two segments address equally if and only if their loader-resolved interpretations are equal. The name says \"\n            [:em \"what the code is\"]\n            \", so it means the same thing on every machine that can hash.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Semantics carries a stamp.\"]\n            \" An address fixes which instructions; it does not fix what \"\n            [:code \":call\"]\n            \" means. So the value carries \"\n            [:code \":yin.k/contract\"]\n            \", naming one published revision of the complete execution contract: opcode table, transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, scheduler semantics. A resumer on a different revision returns \"\n            [:code \":yin.k/profile-mismatch\"]\n            \" before lowering. This is the definition-site environment of the macro, made explicit as a version.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"The grammar is disjoint by construction.\"]\n            \" Every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker. A program's own maps are always wrapped as \"\n            [:code \":yin.k/literal\"]\n            \" with encoded entries. No decoder decision depends on program content, exactly as a hygienic resolver never asks whether a symbol \\\"looks like\\\" a macro temporary. The marks are structural, not heuristic.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"The store slice is isolated, never merged.\"]\n            \" The resumed task runs in a fresh execution store whose contents are exactly the carried reachable slice, with the remapped resource cells and the resumer's own FFI pair held in a private resource table beside it rather than inside it. Nothing of the receiver's participates. A name absent from the slice resolves through primitives and modules exactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to intervene. This is the renaming environment: the transplanted computation sees its own scope and only its own scope.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Primitives are bindings, not meanings.\"]\n            \" A primitive travels as \"\n            [:code \"{:yin.k/primitive :yin.k/name sym}\"]\n            \", and the name is resolved at the resumer under a profile check. A symbol in the environment that referred to a host function is recorded by reverse lookup against the emitter's primitive table, and refused if no portable binding exists. The hygienic system's rule that an identifier is a name plus a binding context is here verbatim.\"]\n           [:li\n            [:strong \"Anything else is refused, totally and by path.\"]\n            \" Encoding is one total function from value and path to outcome. A live handle, a host object, a closure over a non-portable value: each is \"\n            [:code \":yin.k/non-portable\"]\n            \" naming the path where it was found. The lift never produces a half-hygienic value.\"]]\n          [:p\n           \"The structural resemblance is not a metaphor. A hygienic macro system and UCF are solving the same problem on the same abstract object: a fragment that must mean the same thing after transplantation as it did before. The macro system does it for a fragment of syntax entering a new lexical scope. UCF does it for a fragment of execution entering a new machine. Both work by refusing bare names and insisting that every reference carry the context that gives it meaning.\"]\n### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\n\n`dao.lease.md` is explicit that a judge must be composed **inside the\nboundary that possesses the resource** and must reclaim *by acting on what\nit itself holds*; a judge that possesses nothing is outside the contract.\nThe first draft designated \"the composition-designated arbiter for the\ncarrier medium,\" defaulting to the emitter — an arbiter relative to a\ncarrier, possessing an offer, which is a copy of content: the same\noccurrence carried on a second medium would acquire a second arbiter, and\nneither arbiter controls execution of anything. This section replaces that\nwith a grounded design.\n\n**The possessed resource is the occurrence's execution ledger**: the set of\ndatoms about one occurrence id on the arbitration `dao.space` — its offer,\ngrants, renewals, lapses, and resumed reports, plus the occurrence's\n**admission epoch** (§7.7.5). The **grantor is the boundary that transacts\nthat space** — its transactor. Possession is exact and of the right kind:\n\n- the ledger's datom base lives inside the transactor's boundary, and\n  admission to it is the transactor's alone — no other boundary can write\n  those datoms, which is why grantor-authored facts are worth more than\n  holder reports;\n- the **reclaim** is the grantor acting on what it holds: recording the\n  lapse on its own base and advancing the occurrence's epoch datom. It\n  needs no cooperation from any holder and no reach into any carrier;\n- the arbiter is **not medium-relative**: the binding\n  occurrence → arbitration space is minted at first export and travels *in\n  the value* (`:yin.k/arbitration`, §7.2), so every carrier, every snapshot\n  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\n  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\n  by the occurrence, not discovered from wherever a value happened to land;\n- **one holder per occurrence is the grantor's policy, enforced where\n  policy lives**: `dao.lease` does not impose it, and UCF does not add to\n  the vocabulary — the grantor's ledger admits at most one live lease per\n  occurrence (a second grant requires the prior lease reclaimed first,\n  which `dao.lease.md`'s own validity rules make the grantor the sole\n  author of). Exclusive custody is thus a property of the ledger the\n  grantor possesses, not an aspiration over streams it does not.\n\nWhat is possessed, concretely, is the *right to admit effects as this\noccurrence* — the ledger is where epochs and dedup-stamped admissions are\nrecorded, which is what §7.7.5's fencing consults. A composition that\ncannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\n`:yin.k/exclusive` custody at all; it composes `:yin.k/fork` only. That is\nan honest capability limit, not a gap to paper over.\n\n### 7.7.6 Successors and chains\n\nA resumed continuation runs to its next safepoint and either halts or parks\nagain. Either way it produces a successor value — a `:yin.k/continuation`\nor a `:yin.k/result` — whose `:yin.k/origin` carries the predecessor's\n`:yin.k/occurrence` and the lease it ran under. Each park is a **new\noccurrence**: minted fresh by the running holder, chained to its\npredecessor, and itself the subject of the next custody cycle. The holder\nappends the successor, records `:yin.k/resumed` with the successor's\naddress, and releases. The chain of occurrences linked by `:yin.k/origin`\nis the migratory computation's history, queryable on the arbitration\nmedium: where it ran, under which grant, what it became. A rollback is a\nre-offer of an earlier link; effects past that link need compensation, not\nrecomputation (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.4) — and §7.7.5's op-ids\nare what compensation reads to find what was done.\n\n(Amendment r6.) The rollback above is not an automatic transition. A\nclosed occurrence never regains tenure and receives no further grant\n(7.7.8). Re-offering an earlier link, and compensating for effects\npast it, is a governance act outside automatic successor admission.\n\n(M-next C, slice C8.) A halted result completes its occurrence as a\ncontinuation does, but the closure's edge is a terminal edge to the\nresult's address (7.7.2). A result is not an occurrence: it is never\noffered, admitted or granted, no ancestry runs through it, and the\nchain ends there.\n\n### 7.7.7 Restart\n\nA grantor that lost its ledger reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`,\n*Restart*: reclaim first, then grant afresh — a new grant alone does not\nend the prior tenure). Its inventory of possessed resources is the set of\noccurrence ledgers it transacts; its recoverable holder for each is the\n`:dao.lease/holder` of the last grant, both of which are facts on (or the\nrecoverable state of) the space it possesses. Both are queries, which is\nwhy custody lives on a `dao.space` and not on the carrier stream.\n\n(M-next C, slice C6.) An authority that reopens its durable ledger\nruns these steps in order, and serves nothing before the last:\n\n1. Open the ledger and fold it.\n2. Reclaim every tenure it shows live, cause `:policy`, in grant\n   order, each lapse with its epoch change as one transaction; an eligible\n   accepted report also records closure and its exact edge in that transaction\n   (7.7.8). Nothing is regranted.\n3. Rebuild the judge from the ledger after those reclaims: its seen\n   facts from every recorded grant and lapse (a lapse of cause\n   `:release` is the release; no separate release is recorded), its\n   answered proposals from the recorded grants and refusals.\n4. Wire the proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a proposal\n   drained before its occurrence could be granted is judged again.\n\nA reclaim that does not commit refuses the whole open; reopen is\nretried, and replays the ledger to the same state. The reclaim is\nthe adapter contract of `dao.lease.md`, *Composition duties*.\nBecause reopen reclaims, a holder's admission retry after a crash\nanswers `:stale` (7.7.8 step 3); its committed result is recovered\n-           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined connection does not give a globally consistent identification of states when the base is curved, and the failure is measured by holonomy around loops. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They make the path \"\n+           \" story. It says: even a perfectly well-defined, perfectly flat connection gives you one faithful copy per reader, and when the carrier does not consume, that is several candidate sections over one point with nothing to choose between them. Occurrence identity, lease custody, epochs, fenced writers, the ledger as authority. These do not make the connection better defined. They are the gluing condition that makes the world line \"\n            [:strong \"single\"]\n-           \": they ensure that of all the loops the value might be carried around, exactly one closes as the continuation of the computation, and every other closure is distinguishable as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n+           \": of all the candidate continuations over an occurrence, exactly one is admitted as the computation, and every other is distinguishable afterwards as a lapsed incarnation.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree with no loops. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and the manifold they live on has nontrivial topology: partitions, retries, crash restarts, redelivery. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n+           \"Hygienic macro systems never needed the second half because lexical scoping is flat. Expansion happens once, at one place, and the result is a tree: one expansion per site, nothing to glue. Execution state in a distributed system does not have that luxury. The same checkpoint can be read from a stream by any number of peers at any later time, and partitions, retries, crash restarts, and redelivery multiply the readers. UCF needed both halves because it transports an object that macro systems never had to move: something that is simultaneously code, environment, store, and the future of a running process, across a space where \\\"the same place\\\" is not a well-defined notion without a ledger to define it.\"]\n           [:h2 \"Part Three: Transport to a Different Machine\"]\n           [:p\n            \"Everything above moves a continuation between two instances of the same machine on different hosts. The fibers differ in coordinates (tempids, store keys, host representations) but not in kind: every yin.vm holds the canonical CESK and nothing else. The next question is what changes when the destination is not yin.vm at all but a different virtual machine with its own heap, its own values, and its own compiled code. This part records the decisions and, deliberately, the path that led to them, because the first two answers were wrong in instructive ways. One qualifier governs everything below: these are design decisions, not landed code. What ships today is the nested value grammar of Part One; the tuple form described here is the next specification change.\"]\n@@ -331,7 +335,7 @@\n            [:code \":yin.k/id\"]\n            \" is that lesson, with the one necessary refinement that content identity and occurrence identity must be kept apart. Lisp taught, through forty years of macro pain, that a name is not a meaning until it carries its binding context. UCF's primitive markers, contract stamps, and content hashes are that lesson applied to a runtime instead of a reader. Just-in-time compilers taught that compiled code can be abandoned mid-flight and the interpreter's frame rebuilt from metadata kept at safepoints, and that the reverse, on-stack replacement, enters compiled code at those same points. Part Three's lift is deoptimization across a machine boundary, and its lower is analogous to on-stack replacement, though it may simply start a fresh native task from the canonical frame rather than enter a running activation; the tuple set is the interpreter frame they meet at.\"]\n           [:p\n-           \"And differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with, and that on a curved space the best connection in the world still leaves holonomy around loops which you must measure rather than wish away. UCF's seventh section is that lesson, written in datoms.\"]\n+           \"And geometry taught two things. Differential geometry taught that moving a thing without distortion is not a property of the thing but of the connection you move it with. Sheaf theory taught that local pieces, each correct on its own patch, do not add up to a global object unless a gluing condition says how they agree on the overlaps, and that the condition is extra structure which nothing in the pieces supplies. UCF's seventh section is the second lesson, written in datoms: the connection is Parts One and Three, and the ledger is the gluing.\"]\n           [:p\n            \"A continuation is a value, not a session. That is the stance at the head of the UCF specification, and everything else follows from taking it seriously. A value can be content-addressed, so it can be deduplicated and verified. A value can be carried by anything, so no carrier is privileged. A value can be read by anyone, so custody must be arbitrated above the carrier by a boundary that actually possesses something. Hygiene makes the value mean the same thing everywhere. The lease makes sure it runs in only one place at a time. Between them, a suspended computation becomes a vector in a bundle over the distributed world, and UCF is the connection that lets it travel.\"]\n           [:p [:strong \"Learn more:\"]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 16:12:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-09 23:12:24 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReviewed `cc5bf3ed`, the three-commit log, the preceding-commit diff, and the essay from disk. No files edited.\n\nThe recast correctly distinguishes faithful copying from path-dependent transport. Four essay-accuracy issues remain; none requires changing the architecture.\n\n1. **MEDIUM | public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog:239 | Custody does not admit exactly one candidate forever.**  \n   §7.7.7–§7.7.8 permit successive grants for an uncompleted occurrence after reclaim. Admission can also suspend, so there is no unconditional existence guarantee. A candidate that never obtained a grant is not a “lapsed incarnation.” Fork policy deliberately permits multiple continuations.\n\n   **Correction:** Replace the sentence with: “Under exclusive custody, at most one lease is current for an occurrence at a time. Reclaim may admit a replacement holder; stale holders cannot commit protected effects, and completion records exactly one successor or terminal edge.” Scope the abstract’s “one of those readings” and line 194’s “where there must be one” to exclusive custody.\n\n2. **MEDIUM | public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog:217 | The epoch is part of the admission condition, not the whole condition.**  \n   §7.7.8 requires readable authority, a valid authenticated binding, an open occurrence, the active lease, matching epoch, operation scope, and intent/dedup checks. Matching the epoch alone establishes neither tenure nor permission to commit.\n\n   **Correction:** Say “The epoch is one term of that condition, bound by the authority to the grant; admission checks the full binding, tenure, operation scope and intent.” Keep the preceding atomic-admission paragraph.\n\n3. **MEDIUM | public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog:162 | A curvature-dependent explanation remains.**  \n   “The manifold is not flat: hosts differ…” still treats host heterogeneity as evidence of non-flatness, contrary to the new distinction at line 192. Differences in representations and available capabilities do not establish curvature.\n\n   **Correction:** Replace it with “Hosts differ in representation and available capabilities; those differences are what lift, lower and compatibility checks must account for.”\n\n4. **MEDIUM | public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog:194 | The sheaf language is not yet explicitly qualified.**  \n   The text asserts local sections over neighbourhoods and a global section without defining the base, restriction maps or overlap conditions. Line 158 still calls the correspondence “exact enough to compute with.” Moreover, sheaf gluing assembles compatible local sections; it does not itself select one competing candidate. The move from host/time points to occurrence points at line 204 also needs qualification.\n\n   The general-geometry sentence at line 156 is too broad: a flat connection can have global holonomy on a non-simply-connected base. Curvature measures infinitesimal failure, not every possible form of path dependence.\n\n   **Correction:** Add an explicit qualification: “This is a gluing analogy, not a constructed sheaf: the ledger supplies the admissibility rules for local candidate continuations.” Replace line 158’s exactness claim with an analogy qualifier; describe UCF transport as path-independent **up to the canonical equivalence already stated**, rather than proving geometric flatness. At line 156, distinguish curvature from possible global holonomy. Qualify the Lineage’s “ledger is the gluing” accordingly.\n\nPart Three’s references to Part Two’s geometry and custody remain coherent with these corrections. The retained separation between transport and admission, atomic intent/dedup checks, and honest limits on protected effects passes review.\n\nThe retraction sentence at line 192 is acceptable in a published essay and need not be cut. For a more timeless presentation, optionally replace its opening with “This copying should not be mistaken for holonomy.” That is editorial, not blocking.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED"}}
